    {
      "id": 5,
      "title": "MCP for PM",
      "body": "Description pending",
      "status": "done",
      "priority": "high",
      "labels": [
        "mcp"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1788448748,
      "updated": 1788495377,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "reaganiwadha",
          "created": 1788481882,
          "body": "So MCP for PM is needed. The goal for PM is to be more of a diff based review annotate and ticketing way of vibecoding rather than just pure prompting so it's like,,, i hate the word,,, second brain. But the goal is for pm to have an mcp crate and have a stdio mcp that allows it to list projects, open up a project in the pm, list tickets, comment on tickets, read on tickets. Solving this base PM-5 needs to solve PM-15 first.",
          "anchor": null
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788483066,
          "body": "MCP server shipped as crates/pm-mcp (rmcp 3.2, stdio). Works directly on .pm/pm.json5 via pm-core (no GUI needed); project chosen by --project / positional / cwd, with per-call project override that walks up to the nearest .pm/. Tools: list_tickets, get_ticket, add_comment, create_ticket, edit_ticket, open_project (spawns the pm GUI), list_projects (dir scan). .mcp.json at the repo root registers it with Claude Code. This comment was posted through pm-mcp itself. Depends on PM-15 (author), also in_progress.",
          "anchor": null
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "author": "reaganiwadha",
          "created": 1788486266,
          "body": "We need MCP Installer. install.ps1 installs the pm binary yes, but we should have a subcommand named `pm setup`. Actually let's change the cli syntax to be `pm .` to open this project `pm` just opens pm without specifying directory, `pm --mcp` to run mcp `pm --setup` is the first thing the install.ps1 launches. and in this pm --setup, it asks whether to associate itself/add itself in the menu, register like windows application registry properly, register an uninstaller so it shows up in the bcuninstaller/windows uninstall thingy. and more importantly, pm --setup has an mcp configurator/mcp plugger. Find like a repository where it has an mcp plugger that detects claude code or something and plugs itself properly. Target claude code first",
          "anchor": null
        },
        {
          "id": 4,
          "author": "unknown",
          "created": 1788495095,
          "body": "Closed, MCP primitives is there now",
          "anchor": null
        },
        {
          "id": 5,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788495377,
          "body": "Done. Recap of everything shipped for this ticket:\n\n- crates/pm-mcp — rmcp 3.2 stdio server; tools list_tickets / get_ticket / add_comment / create_ticket / edit_ticket / open_project / list_projects. Works directly on .pm/pm.json5 via pm-core.\n- CLI reshaped to flags (hard switch): `pm` = \"Nothing opened\" screen, `pm .` / `pm <path>` = GUI, `pm --mcp [--project p]`, `pm --setup [--yes]`, `pm --uninstall [--yes]`, `pm --update`.\n- `pm --setup` (crates/pm-core/src/setup.rs, Windows): figlet banner + plain y/n prompts; reg.exe writes App Paths + Uninstall keys (pm shows in \"Installed apps\" / BCUninstaller, UninstallString = \"<exe>\" --uninstall --yes); ensures install dir on PATH; optional Start Menu .lnk; MCP plugger targets Claude Code — `claude mcp add pm -s user -- pm --mcp` if the CLI is present, else edits ~/.claude.json mcpServers.pm.\n- `pm --uninstall` reverses all of it and self-deletes.\n- install.ps1 runs `pm --setup` after install; repo .mcp.json uses `cargo run -q -p pm -- --mcp`.\n\nFollow-ups worth their own tickets: MCP plugger support for Cursor / Windsurf; `pm --setup` on macOS/Linux (currently Windows-only, bails elsewhere).",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "title": "Tickets: wire line-anchored comments from the diff gutter",
      "body": "Hovering a diff line should offer \"comment on lines N–M\", creating a Comment (or Ticket) carrying an `Anchor { file, start_line, end_line, rev }`.\n\n## No longer blocked\nThe 2025 comment on this ticket said \"blocked on a real text-input widget — the current one is a stopgap\". That widget shipped: **PM-72** (commit 3925d0c) moved `TextInput` into `crates/fremantle/src/text_input.rs` (1056 lines — full IME via `EntityInputHandler` :636-741, single/multiline, selection, undo/redo). The blocker is gone.\n\n## What already exists\n- **The data model is complete.** `Anchor { file, start_line, end_line, rev }` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:108-116, `rev` is a short commit id or the literal `\"working\"`), `Ticket.anchors: Vec<Anchor>` (:189), and `Comment.anchor: Option<Anchor>` (:132). The model's own doc (:104) says \"Not created by the UI yet — the diff-gutter flow comes later\", which is still accurate.\n- **Anchors already render**, read-only, in the ticket detail pane as `📎 file:start–end` (tickets_view.rs:662-683).\n- `AppState::add_comment` (state.rs:243) already takes an `Option<Anchor>`.\n\nSo the write path is largely built. What's missing is the diff-side UI to create one, and the read-side jump to actually use one.\n\n## Split this into two slices\n\n**Slice 1 — make existing anchors jump correctly (small, independently useful).**\nClicking an anchor today sets `view = View::Files` and calls `open_path(file)` — but `open_path` (app.rs:435-445) **resets both scroll offsets to zero**, so it opens the file at line 1 and the anchor's line range is ignored entirely. Every anchor in the system is currently a file link pretending to be a line link.\n\nThe fix has a template: `scroll_to_first_change` (app.rs:643-661) already finds a target row, sets `diff.y.offset.y = (i - 3) * row_h`, clamps, and drops a caret there. Generalise it to `scroll_to_line(n)` and call it after `open_path`. Note the row-index mapping — `Anchor` line numbers are 1-based file lines, while `diff.y.offset` indexes the side-by-side `rows` vec (`side_by_side`, pm-core/src/diff.rs:25-53), and those diverge wherever the other side has inserted rows. `scroll_to_first_change` sidesteps this by searching `rows` directly; an anchor jump can't.\n\n**Slice 2 — create anchors from the gutter.**\nHover a diff row → an affordance in the gutter → select a range → a composer. Relevant machinery: the diff body is a custom `Element` with its own mouse handling (`register_mouse`, diff_view.rs:836-877) and existing drag-selection, so it already knows which row the pointer is on; `MAX_ROWS` truncation (state.rs:16) means an anchor can point past what's rendered.\n\n`rev` should come from `state.target` (`DiffTarget::WorkingTree` → `\"working\"`, `Commit(oid)` → the short id) — `CommitInfo.short_id` (git.rs:32) is right there.\n\n## Open questions\n- Comment on a ticket, or **new** ticket from the gutter? The title says comment; in practice \"file a ticket about this line\" is the more common want, and `Ticket.anchors` is a `Vec`, suggesting that was the original intent. Possibly both, but decide before building the composer.\n- Which ticket does a gutter comment attach to? There's no \"current ticket\" concept in the Files view — this needs a picker, which is real UI.\n- Anchors are captured against a `rev` but nothing re-anchors them as the file changes. A `\"working\"` anchor is stale the moment the file is edited. Out of scope here, but worth stating so it isn't discovered later.\n\n## Verify\nSlice 1: click an anchor on this very ticket (it has one — `crates/pm-ui/src/diff_view.rs:698-970`) and confirm it lands on line 698, not line 1.\nSlice 2: create an anchored comment from the gutter, confirm it round-trips through .pm/pm.json5 and `mcp__pm__get_ticket`, and that clicking it jumps back to the right line.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "high",
      "labels": [
        "tickets",
        "mcp"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1756900100,
      "updated": 1788622665,
      "anchors": [
        {
          "file": "crates/pm-ui/src/diff_view.rs",
          "start_line": 698,
          "end_line": 970,
          "rev": "working"
        }
      ],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "reaganiwadha",
          "created": 1756900200,
          "body": "Blocked on a real text-input widget — the current one is a stopgap.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622665,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "Next pass. Hovering a diff line should offer 'comment on lines N–M',\ncreating a Comment (or Ticket) with an Anchor { file, start_line, end_line, rev }.",
          "new": "Hovering a diff line should offer \"comment on lines N–M\", creating a Comment (or Ticket) carrying an `Anchor { file, start_line, end_line, rev }`.\n\n## No longer blocked\nThe 2025 comment on this ticket said \"blocked on a real text-input widget — the current one is a stopgap\". That widget shipped: **PM-72** (commit 3925d0c) moved `TextInput` into `crates/fremantle/src/text_input.rs` (1056 lines — full IME via `EntityInputHandler` :636-741, single/multiline, selection, undo/redo). The blocker is gone.\n\n## What already exists\n- **The data model is complete.** `Anchor { file, start_line, end_line, rev }` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:108-116, `rev` is a short commit id or the literal `\"working\"`), `Ticket.anchors: Vec<Anchor>` (:189), and `Comment.anchor: Option<Anchor>` (:132). The model's own doc (:104) says \"Not created by the UI yet — the diff-gutter flow comes later\", which is still accurate.\n- **Anchors already render**, read-only, in the ticket detail pane as `📎 file:start–end` (tickets_view.rs:662-683).\n- `AppState::add_comment` (state.rs:243) already takes an `Option<Anchor>`.\n\nSo the write path is largely built. What's missing is the diff-side UI to create one, and the read-side jump to actually use one.\n\n## Split this into two slices\n\n**Slice 1 — make existing anchors jump correctly (small, independently useful).**\nClicking an anchor today sets `view = View::Files` and calls `open_path(file)` — but `open_path` (app.rs:435-445) **resets both scroll offsets to zero**, so it opens the file at line 1 and the anchor's line range is ignored entirely. Every anchor in the system is currently a file link pretending to be a line link.\n\nThe fix has a template: `scroll_to_first_change` (app.rs:643-661) already finds a target row, sets `diff.y.offset.y = (i - 3) * row_h`, clamps, and drops a caret there. Generalise it to `scroll_to_line(n)` and call it after `open_path`. Note the row-index mapping — `Anchor` line numbers are 1-based file lines, while `diff.y.offset` indexes the side-by-side `rows` vec (`side_by_side`, pm-core/src/diff.rs:25-53), and those diverge wherever the other side has inserted rows. `scroll_to_first_change` sidesteps this by searching `rows` directly; an anchor jump can't.\n\n**Slice 2 — create anchors from the gutter.**\nHover a diff row → an affordance in the gutter → select a range → a composer. Relevant machinery: the diff body is a custom `Element` with its own mouse handling (`register_mouse`, diff_view.rs:836-877) and existing drag-selection, so it already knows which row the pointer is on; `MAX_ROWS` truncation (state.rs:16) means an anchor can point past what's rendered.\n\n`rev` should come from `state.target` (`DiffTarget::WorkingTree` → `\"working\"`, `Commit(oid)` → the short id) — `CommitInfo.short_id` (git.rs:32) is right there.\n\n## Open questions\n- Comment on a ticket, or **new** ticket from the gutter? The title says comment; in practice \"file a ticket about this line\" is the more common want, and `Ticket.anchors` is a `Vec`, suggesting that was the original intent. Possibly both, but decide before building the composer.\n- Which ticket does a gutter comment attach to? There's no \"current ticket\" concept in the Files view — this needs a picker, which is real UI.\n- Anchors are captured against a `rev` but nothing re-anchors them as the file changes. A `\"working\"` anchor is stale the moment the file is edited. Out of scope here, but worth stating so it isn't discovered later.\n\n## Verify\nSlice 1: click an anchor on this very ticket (it has one — `crates/pm-ui/src/diff_view.rs:698-970`) and confirm it lands on line 698, not line 1.\nSlice 2: create an anchored comment from the gutter, confirm it round-trips through .pm/pm.json5 and `mcp__pm__get_ticket`, and that clicking it jumps back to the right line."
        }
      ]
    },
