    {
      "id": 8,
      "title": "Rich ticket formatting using Markdown",
      "body": "Wire the markdown engine from **PM-9** into the ticket pane. This ticket is the consumer; PM-9 is the engine.\n\n## What exists today\nTicket bodies and comment bodies both render through `text_block` (tickets_view.rs:35-44) — split on `\\n`, one `div` per line, single colour, no inline styling. Call sites: body at :657-659, comments at :710-738.\n\nThe irony is measurable: the bodies in this project's own pm.json5 are **already written in markdown** — headings, `**bold**`, backticked `file:line` references, bullet lists — and every one of them currently renders as literal asterisks and hashes.\n\n## In scope\n1. Swap `text_block` for the PM-9 renderer at both call sites.\n2. **Wrapping.** `text_block`'s one-div-per-line model means long lines don't wrap at all today. Ticket bodies in a resizable sidebar-width pane need real wrapping — this is arguably the bigger usability win over the styling.\n3. **Editing.** Bodies are read-only (there is no `AppState::set_body` wrapper — PM-85), so the editor is a plain-text `TextInput::multi` over the markdown source with a rendered read view, the standard split. Decide whether editing is in this ticket or follows PM-57's title-edit pattern separately; recommend keeping this ticket read-only and letting PM-57's approach settle first.\n\n## Depends on\n- **PM-9** (the engine) — hard blocker.\n- **PM-85** (AppState mutators) — only if editing lands here.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-57** (title edit) sets the click-to-edit pattern this should follow.\n- **PM-50 option (c)** — linkifying `PM-N` mentions rides on the same inline pass; the cross-references in these bodies are exactly what would benefit.\n- **PM-33** (image attachments) is the natural next consumer once inline images are wanted.\n\n## Open question\nDoes the comment composer get a preview, or is the rendered view after posting enough? Preview means a second rendering surface and a toggle; for comments that are usually two sentences, probably not worth it.\n\n## Verify\nOpen PM-55 or PM-85 in the GUI — both are long, heavily formatted, and full of backticked paths. Headings, bold, code spans and bullets should all render, long lines should wrap at the pane width, and nothing should be dropped. Cross-check against the raw text via `mcp__pm__get_ticket` to confirm the source is unchanged — this is a rendering change only, the stored body stays markdown.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "tickets"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1788448814,
      "updated": 1788622621,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622621,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "",
          "new": "Wire the markdown engine from **PM-9** into the ticket pane. This ticket is the consumer; PM-9 is the engine.\n\n## What exists today\nTicket bodies and comment bodies both render through `text_block` (tickets_view.rs:35-44) — split on `\\n`, one `div` per line, single colour, no inline styling. Call sites: body at :657-659, comments at :710-738.\n\nThe irony is measurable: the bodies in this project's own pm.json5 are **already written in markdown** — headings, `**bold**`, backticked `file:line` references, bullet lists — and every one of them currently renders as literal asterisks and hashes.\n\n## In scope\n1. Swap `text_block` for the PM-9 renderer at both call sites.\n2. **Wrapping.** `text_block`'s one-div-per-line model means long lines don't wrap at all today. Ticket bodies in a resizable sidebar-width pane need real wrapping — this is arguably the bigger usability win over the styling.\n3. **Editing.** Bodies are read-only (there is no `AppState::set_body` wrapper — PM-85), so the editor is a plain-text `TextInput::multi` over the markdown source with a rendered read view, the standard split. Decide whether editing is in this ticket or follows PM-57's title-edit pattern separately; recommend keeping this ticket read-only and letting PM-57's approach settle first.\n\n## Depends on\n- **PM-9** (the engine) — hard blocker.\n- **PM-85** (AppState mutators) — only if editing lands here.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-57** (title edit) sets the click-to-edit pattern this should follow.\n- **PM-50 option (c)** — linkifying `PM-N` mentions rides on the same inline pass; the cross-references in these bodies are exactly what would benefit.\n- **PM-33** (image attachments) is the natural next consumer once inline images are wanted.\n\n## Open question\nDoes the comment composer get a preview, or is the rendered view after posting enough? Preview means a second rendering surface and a toggle; for comments that are usually two sentences, probably not worth it.\n\n## Verify\nOpen PM-55 or PM-85 in the GUI — both are long, heavily formatted, and full of backticked paths. Headings, bold, code spans and bullets should all render, long lines should wrap at the pane width, and nothing should be dropped. Cross-check against the raw text via `mcp__pm__get_ticket` to confirm the source is unchanged — this is a rendering change only, the stored body stays markdown."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "title": "Markdown viewer engine",
      "body": "The enabler for PM-8 (markdown ticket bodies). Split out because the engine is reusable and the ticket-pane wiring is not.\n\n## What exists today\n**No markdown rendering anywhere in the codebase.** Grepping for `markdown` across `crates/` returns only a `.md → book.svg` file-icon mapping (pm-ui/src/icons.rs:94, 202, 314) and a doc comment noting that GitHub release bodies *are* markdown while being rendered as raw lines (pm-core/src/update.rs:20).\n\nEverything renders as plain text through `text_block` (tickets_view.rs:35-44), which splits on `\\n` and emits one `div` per line — that exists because gpui doesn't break on newlines on its own. No inline styling, one colour, no links, no code blocks.\n\n## What's already available to build on\nThe styled-run machinery is present and proven:\n- `runs_for_line` (diff_view.rs:113-132) already builds per-span `TextRun`s from `pm_core::highlight::Line`, with `to_hsla` (:19-21) for colour conversion. Two more sites do the same: list_view.rs:148-179 (two-colour badge in one shaped line) and tree_view.rs:134-142.\n- `TextRun` carries `font`, `color`, `background_color`, `underline`, `strikethrough` — **the bold/italic/underline fields are entirely unused today**. Bold and italic come from the `font` field, which means the fonts have to be registered (`src/fonts.rs`) before bold text can render; check what's loaded before assuming it works.\n- Syntax highlighting for fenced code blocks already exists — `pm-core/src/highlight.rs` (syntect) produces `Line = Vec<Span { text, color }>`, the same shape a code block would want.\n\n## Scope\nA parser + a renderer producing gpui elements. Two decisions to make first:\n\n**1. Parser: pull a crate or hand-roll?** `pulldown-cmark` is the obvious dependency and handles CommonMark properly. Against it: the workspace currently keeps its dependency list very short (fremantle depends on `gpui` alone). A hand-rolled subset covering bold/italic/inline code/links/headings/lists/fences is maybe 200 lines and covers everything ticket bodies actually use. Recommend the crate — markdown is a bad thing to half-implement, and the failure mode is silently mangling someone's ticket.\n\n**2. Where does it live?** If it renders gpui elements it belongs in `crates/fremantle` (it's domain-agnostic, and PM-79 wants a doc viewer built out of fremantle, which is the same machinery). If it's a pure text→spans pass with no gpui, `pm-core` alongside `highlight.rs` fits better. Recommend fremantle — the wrapping and layout are the hard parts and they're inherently gpui.\n\n## Known consumers\n1. **PM-8** — ticket bodies and comment bodies (tickets_view.rs:657-659, :710-738).\n2. **Release notes** — currently printed line-by-line unstyled in the About overlay (app.rs:1597-1601), and they are genuinely markdown (`release-notes/*.md`, embedded via pm-core/build.rs).\n3. **PM-50 option (c)** — linkifying `PM-N` mentions is the same inline-span pass.\n4. **PM-79** — a rustdoc viewer built out of fremantle needs exactly this.\n\nFour consumers is a good sign the split from PM-8 is right.\n\n## Scope boundary\nTicket bodies need: paragraphs, bold/italic, inline code, fenced code, links, bullet and numbered lists, headings. They do **not** need: tables, footnotes, HTML passthrough, images (that's PM-33). Start with the first set; a partial renderer that ignores what it doesn't know is fine as long as it degrades to plain text rather than dropping content.\n\n## Verify\nRender this project's own ticket bodies — PM-55, PM-85 and PM-86 all use headings, bold, inline code, bullet lists and `file:line` references — and confirm nothing is dropped or mangled. Unit-test the parser separately from the rendering. Then check the About overlay's release notes as the second consumer.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1788448819,
      "updated": 1788622610,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622610,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "",
          "new": "The enabler for PM-8 (markdown ticket bodies). Split out because the engine is reusable and the ticket-pane wiring is not.\n\n## What exists today\n**No markdown rendering anywhere in the codebase.** Grepping for `markdown` across `crates/` returns only a `.md → book.svg` file-icon mapping (pm-ui/src/icons.rs:94, 202, 314) and a doc comment noting that GitHub release bodies *are* markdown while being rendered as raw lines (pm-core/src/update.rs:20).\n\nEverything renders as plain text through `text_block` (tickets_view.rs:35-44), which splits on `\\n` and emits one `div` per line — that exists because gpui doesn't break on newlines on its own. No inline styling, one colour, no links, no code blocks.\n\n## What's already available to build on\nThe styled-run machinery is present and proven:\n- `runs_for_line` (diff_view.rs:113-132) already builds per-span `TextRun`s from `pm_core::highlight::Line`, with `to_hsla` (:19-21) for colour conversion. Two more sites do the same: list_view.rs:148-179 (two-colour badge in one shaped line) and tree_view.rs:134-142.\n- `TextRun` carries `font`, `color`, `background_color`, `underline`, `strikethrough` — **the bold/italic/underline fields are entirely unused today**. Bold and italic come from the `font` field, which means the fonts have to be registered (`src/fonts.rs`) before bold text can render; check what's loaded before assuming it works.\n- Syntax highlighting for fenced code blocks already exists — `pm-core/src/highlight.rs` (syntect) produces `Line = Vec<Span { text, color }>`, the same shape a code block would want.\n\n## Scope\nA parser + a renderer producing gpui elements. Two decisions to make first:\n\n**1. Parser: pull a crate or hand-roll?** `pulldown-cmark` is the obvious dependency and handles CommonMark properly. Against it: the workspace currently keeps its dependency list very short (fremantle depends on `gpui` alone). A hand-rolled subset covering bold/italic/inline code/links/headings/lists/fences is maybe 200 lines and covers everything ticket bodies actually use. Recommend the crate — markdown is a bad thing to half-implement, and the failure mode is silently mangling someone's ticket.\n\n**2. Where does it live?** If it renders gpui elements it belongs in `crates/fremantle` (it's domain-agnostic, and PM-79 wants a doc viewer built out of fremantle, which is the same machinery). If it's a pure text→spans pass with no gpui, `pm-core` alongside `highlight.rs` fits better. Recommend fremantle — the wrapping and layout are the hard parts and they're inherently gpui.\n\n## Known consumers\n1. **PM-8** — ticket bodies and comment bodies (tickets_view.rs:657-659, :710-738).\n2. **Release notes** — currently printed line-by-line unstyled in the About overlay (app.rs:1597-1601), and they are genuinely markdown (`release-notes/*.md`, embedded via pm-core/build.rs).\n3. **PM-50 option (c)** — linkifying `PM-N` mentions is the same inline-span pass.\n4. **PM-79** — a rustdoc viewer built out of fremantle needs exactly this.\n\nFour consumers is a good sign the split from PM-8 is right.\n\n## Scope boundary\nTicket bodies need: paragraphs, bold/italic, inline code, fenced code, links, bullet and numbered lists, headings. They do **not** need: tables, footnotes, HTML passthrough, images (that's PM-33). Start with the first set; a partial renderer that ignores what it doesn't know is fine as long as it degrades to plain text rather than dropping content.\n\n## Verify\nRender this project's own ticket bodies — PM-55, PM-85 and PM-86 all use headings, bold, inline code, bullet lists and `file:line` references — and confirm nothing is dropped or mangled. Unit-test the parser separately from the rendering. Then check the About overlay's release notes as the second consumer."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 15,
      "title": "Author of tickets and author of comments, so someone via MCP can pose as someone, and someone with pm gui can pose as someone",
      "body": "",
      "status": "done",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "mcp"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1788449204,
      "updated": 1788584041,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788495425,
          "body": "Partial. Done: Ticket.author / Comment.author (free, unverified strings), Config.author (~/.pm/config.json), pm_core::resolve_author(explicit → config → git user.name → \"unknown\"), the GUI \"as:\" author_box in the Tickets pane, and per-action Option<String> overrides in create_ticket / add_comment (MCP tools take `author`).\n\nNot done: a first-class \"who am I\" control. PM-56 covers that (footer user section, git-username default). Also a bug to fix there: a non-git pm window resolves author to \"unknown\" and the ticket-submit path then persists that into the shared config, poisoning attribution for every project — the default should come from git user.name and only be written to config on an explicit user action.",
          "anchor": null
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788584041,
          "body": "Done via PM-56: the status-bar \"Acting as\" section is the first-class who-am-I control (git username by default, free-text override, persisted to Config.author). Also fixed the poisoning bug: an empty window no longer writes \"unknown\" back into the shared config — load_repo re-seeds the identity field from the freshly resolved git name instead of leaving it stale.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": []
    },
