    {
      "id": 18,
      "title": "Color scheming: a second palette + a way to pick it (PM-71 landed the infra only)",
      "body": "PM-71's commit message says \"closes PM-18\", but only the **infrastructure** landed. Keeping this open for the user-facing half.\n\n## What exists now (after PM-71, commit f005df5)\n`crates/fremantle/src/theme.rs` (107 lines) has a real structured theme: `ThemeColors` (10 fields, :22-33), `ThemeMetrics` (23 fields, :37-62), `Theme` (:65-71) with `rm()`/`scale_of()` for zoom, installed once as a `GlobalTheme` (:89) and read via the `ActiveTheme` ext trait (:99) — the same `cx.theme()` pattern Zed uses. pm-ui builds its own palette in `crates/pm-ui/src/theme.rs` (73 lines) and installs it in `Pm::new`.\n\n## What's missing — the actual \"scheming\"\n- **Only one palette exists.** `Appearance` (theme.rs:14) has `Dark`/`Light` variants, but its own doc comment (:11) says it is \"informational for now (no built-in light/dark pair is shipped)\". pm-ui defines a single dark palette; nothing constructs a light one.\n- **No way to select a scheme.** No theme picker in the menu bar (`menu.rs`), no `theme` key in `Config` (`pm-core/src/config.rs:26-48` has `ui_scale`, `author`, `recent`, `watchjump`, `stores` — no theme), so a choice couldn't persist even if the UI existed.\n- **No theme loading.** No serde on `Theme`, no theme-file format, no registry of named themes. Every palette must be compiled in.\n- **Some colors bypass the theme entirely.** `Status::color()` (pm-core/src/pm.rs:49) and `Priority::color()` (:94) return raw `u32` hex from the *data* layer, and `ChangeStatus::color()` (git.rs:65) does the same; `list_view.rs:18-19` hardcodes `ADD_FG`/`DEL_FG` outside the theme. A light scheme would leave all of these unreadable. See the fremantle pm-isms ticket, which tracks the same leak from the other direction.\n\n## Suggested scope for this ticket\n1. Build a light `ThemeColors` alongside the dark one in pm-ui.\n2. Move the bypassing colors (status, priority, change status, add/del fg) onto `ThemeColors` so they switch with the rest.\n3. Add `theme: String` (or an `Appearance`) to `Config` and a picker — the menu-bar dropdown (`menu.rs:menu_groups`, which already renders checkmarks) is the cheapest host; persist via `ConfigStore::update` (pm-ui/src/config.rs:113-137) exactly like `watchjump`/`ui_scale` do.\n\nLoadable theme files are explicitly **out of scope** here — worth its own ticket if it's ever wanted.\n\n## Open question\nFollow the system appearance automatically, or is an explicit user choice enough for v1?\n\n## Verify\n`cargo check`; switch schemes at runtime and confirm every surface repaints — in particular the diff add/del row tints, the ticket status dots and priority chips, and the file-tree changed-file tint, since those are the ones fed by non-theme colors today.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1788449551,
      "updated": 1788622374,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622374,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "title_changed",
          "old": "Color Scheming System",
          "new": "Color scheming: a second palette + a way to pick it (PM-71 landed the infra only)"
        },
        {
          "at": 1788622374,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "Very low priority",
          "new": "PM-71's commit message says \"closes PM-18\", but only the **infrastructure** landed. Keeping this open for the user-facing half.\n\n## What exists now (after PM-71, commit f005df5)\n`crates/fremantle/src/theme.rs` (107 lines) has a real structured theme: `ThemeColors` (10 fields, :22-33), `ThemeMetrics` (23 fields, :37-62), `Theme` (:65-71) with `rm()`/`scale_of()` for zoom, installed once as a `GlobalTheme` (:89) and read via the `ActiveTheme` ext trait (:99) — the same `cx.theme()` pattern Zed uses. pm-ui builds its own palette in `crates/pm-ui/src/theme.rs` (73 lines) and installs it in `Pm::new`.\n\n## What's missing — the actual \"scheming\"\n- **Only one palette exists.** `Appearance` (theme.rs:14) has `Dark`/`Light` variants, but its own doc comment (:11) says it is \"informational for now (no built-in light/dark pair is shipped)\". pm-ui defines a single dark palette; nothing constructs a light one.\n- **No way to select a scheme.** No theme picker in the menu bar (`menu.rs`), no `theme` key in `Config` (`pm-core/src/config.rs:26-48` has `ui_scale`, `author`, `recent`, `watchjump`, `stores` — no theme), so a choice couldn't persist even if the UI existed.\n- **No theme loading.** No serde on `Theme`, no theme-file format, no registry of named themes. Every palette must be compiled in.\n- **Some colors bypass the theme entirely.** `Status::color()` (pm-core/src/pm.rs:49) and `Priority::color()` (:94) return raw `u32` hex from the *data* layer, and `ChangeStatus::color()` (git.rs:65) does the same; `list_view.rs:18-19` hardcodes `ADD_FG`/`DEL_FG` outside the theme. A light scheme would leave all of these unreadable. See the fremantle pm-isms ticket, which tracks the same leak from the other direction.\n\n## Suggested scope for this ticket\n1. Build a light `ThemeColors` alongside the dark one in pm-ui.\n2. Move the bypassing colors (status, priority, change status, add/del fg) onto `ThemeColors` so they switch with the rest.\n3. Add `theme: String` (or an `Appearance`) to `Config` and a picker — the menu-bar dropdown (`menu.rs:menu_groups`, which already renders checkmarks) is the cheapest host; persist via `ConfigStore::update` (pm-ui/src/config.rs:113-137) exactly like `watchjump`/`ui_scale` do.\n\nLoadable theme files are explicitly **out of scope** here — worth its own ticket if it's ever wanted.\n\n## Open question\nFollow the system appearance automatically, or is an explicit user choice enough for v1?\n\n## Verify\n`cargo check`; switch schemes at runtime and confirm every surface repaints — in particular the diff add/del row tints, the ticket status dots and priority chips, and the file-tree changed-file tint, since those are the ones fed by non-theme colors today."
        }
      ]
    },
