      "id": 27,
      "title": "Related to PM-14, add a github actions for building in the release branch",
      "body": "Add a way to carry release notes and changes in a release and make sure it's embedded in the binary so we can see it in about",
      "status": "done",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1788474144,
      "updated": 1788495405,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788484542,
          "body": ".github/workflows/release.yml added: triggers on tag v*, builds on windows-latest, guards that the tag matches the root Cargo.toml version and that release-notes/<version>.md exists, then cargo build --release -p pm, sha256, and softprops/action-gh-release with body_path = the release-notes file. Release notes are carried as release-notes/<version>.md (release-notes/0.0.1.md seeded). They are embedded in the binary by crates/pm-core/build.rs -> pm_core::buildinfo (VERSION / COMMIT / DATE / RELEASE_NOTES); the About box shows long_version() = \"pm 0.0.1 (<sha>, <date>)\" plus a scrollable notes block, and pm --version prints the same. Workflow: bump Cargo.toml version + add the notes file, then tag.",
          "anchor": null
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788484747,
          "body": ".github/workflows/release.yml added: trigger on tag v*, build on windows-latest, guard (tag == root Cargo.toml version AND release-notes/<version>.md exists), cargo build --release -p pm, sha256, softprops/action-gh-release with body_path = the notes file. Notes live as release-notes/<version>.md (0.0.1 seeded) and are embedded by crates/pm-core/build.rs -> pm_core::buildinfo (VERSION/COMMIT/DATE/RELEASE_NOTES). About shows long_version() = 'pm 0.0.1 (<sha>, <date>)' + a scrollable notes block; pm --version prints the same. Release flow: bump Cargo.toml version + add the notes file, then tag. Also fixed a concurrency bug found while dogfooding: rmcp runs tool calls in parallel and two writes in a batch clobbered each other -> added a process-wide STORE_LOCK around every read-modify-write in pm-mcp ops.",
          "anchor": null
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788495368,
          "body": "Done. .github/workflows/release.yml: push tag v* → windows-latest → \"Resolve version\" guard (tag == root Cargo.toml version, release-notes/<v>.md must exist) → cargo build --release -p pm → certutil SHA256 → softprops/action-gh-release with target/release/pm.exe + pm.exe.sha256, body_path release-notes/<v>.md. Exercised by v0.0.1, v0.0.2, v0.0.3. The embedded RELEASE_NOTES (crates/pm-core/build.rs → buildinfo) render in the About box. Bump the 4 crate versions + add release-notes/<v>.md before tagging.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": []
    },
    {
      "id": 28,
      "title": "Can't tab between inputs like Title to Description",
      "body": "",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1788474164,
      "updated": 1788622720,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788622720,
          "body": "Keeping this open as the user-facing symptom; the implementation is **PM-84** (part 3/3 of PM-81), which closes this when it lands.\n\nRoot cause, for the record: there is no `Tab` binding anywhere in the repo — only `ctrl-tab`/`ctrl-shift-tab` for top-level view cycling (src/main.rs:89-90) — and no focus-order concept at all. The two inputs in question are `new_ticket_title` and `new_ticket_body`, both built in `Pm::new` (app.rs:210-217); each owns a `FocusHandle` and can be focused imperatively, but nothing ever moves focus *between* them.\n\nSee PM-84 for the constraints (a key context is needed first, or a `Tab` binding would fire inside the diff view too).",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622720,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "commented",
          "comment_id": 1
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 29,
      "title": "Sort tickets by Priority, Latest Change, etc",
      "body": "## What exists today\n**There is no sorting at all, anywhere.** `shown_tickets` (tickets_view.rs:274-286) iterates `self.state.pm.tickets` in raw order and only *filters* — by the status set (`ticket_filter`, app.rs:182) and by PM-75's search string. It never sorts.\n\nThat raw order is the pm.json5 array order, and `create_ticket` pushes to the end (pm-core/src/pm.rs:378), so the list is effectively **creation order, ascending, forever**. The oldest ticket is always on top. `t.created` and `t.updated` are both stored and `updated` is even rendered in the detail pane as `updated {rel_time}` (:638-643) — neither is ever used as a key.\n\nThere is also no `order`/`rank`/`position` field on `Ticket` (pm.rs:165-197), so manual drag-ordering isn't available without a schema change (and would fight the MCP writer, which mutates the same Vec — pm-mcp/src/ops.rs).\n\n## In scope\nA sort control in the list header (tickets_view.rs:319-363, which already holds the search toggle, `filter_button` and `+ New`), offering at least: Priority, Last updated, Created, and probably Status. Reuse `menu_panel` (:123-180) — it already renders a checkmark column, which suits a single-choice picker if only one row is ever checked. Follow the popover mutual-exclusion convention (:252-254).\n\nSorting happens in `shown_tickets` after filtering. Two things to get right there:\n- **A stable tiebreaker.** Sorting by priority alone leaves ~50 Normal tickets in arbitrary relative order; fall back to `updated` or `id` so the list doesn't reshuffle between frames.\n- **`Priority` needs an ordering.** It derives `PartialEq, Eq` but not `Ord` (pm.rs:75-79), and the variant declaration order is Low → Normal → High → Urgent, so a derived `Ord` would sort ascending-by-urgency — i.e. exactly backwards from what anyone wants by default. Decide deliberately rather than deriving and hoping.\n\n**Persist the choice.** `ConfigStore::update` (pm-ui/src/config.rs:113-137) already persists `ui_scale`, `watchjump`, `author` and `recent` to ~/.pm/config.json; add a sort key the same way. Note this is a *user* preference, so it belongs in `Config`, not in pm.json5 where it would be committed and shared.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-4** asked for this in its comment (\"we need a sorting system or categorization system\"). Sorting by priority is only useful once priority is visible in the row, which is PM-4's other half — worth doing them together.\n- **PM-63** (make active filter/sort obvious) — a sort that silently persists across restarts is confusing unless the header says what it is.\n- **PM-62** (kanban) needs grouping, which is the same machinery pointed sideways; whatever `shown_tickets` grows here should be reusable there.\n\n## Open question\nShould the default change? Oldest-first is a defensible default for a tracker you read top-to-bottom, but with 55 open tickets it buries everything recent. Recommend defaulting to `updated` descending and letting the config remember otherwise.\n\n## Verify\nSwitch sort modes with a mixed set of priorities and statuses, confirm the order is correct and stable (no reshuffling on repaint), that it composes with both the status filter and the search box rather than replacing them, and that the choice survives an app restart.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1788474195,
      "updated": 1788622529,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622529,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "",
          "new": "## What exists today\n**There is no sorting at all, anywhere.** `shown_tickets` (tickets_view.rs:274-286) iterates `self.state.pm.tickets` in raw order and only *filters* — by the status set (`ticket_filter`, app.rs:182) and by PM-75's search string. It never sorts.\n\nThat raw order is the pm.json5 array order, and `create_ticket` pushes to the end (pm-core/src/pm.rs:378), so the list is effectively **creation order, ascending, forever**. The oldest ticket is always on top. `t.created` and `t.updated` are both stored and `updated` is even rendered in the detail pane as `updated {rel_time}` (:638-643) — neither is ever used as a key.\n\nThere is also no `order`/`rank`/`position` field on `Ticket` (pm.rs:165-197), so manual drag-ordering isn't available without a schema change (and would fight the MCP writer, which mutates the same Vec — pm-mcp/src/ops.rs).\n\n## In scope\nA sort control in the list header (tickets_view.rs:319-363, which already holds the search toggle, `filter_button` and `+ New`), offering at least: Priority, Last updated, Created, and probably Status. Reuse `menu_panel` (:123-180) — it already renders a checkmark column, which suits a single-choice picker if only one row is ever checked. Follow the popover mutual-exclusion convention (:252-254).\n\nSorting happens in `shown_tickets` after filtering. Two things to get right there:\n- **A stable tiebreaker.** Sorting by priority alone leaves ~50 Normal tickets in arbitrary relative order; fall back to `updated` or `id` so the list doesn't reshuffle between frames.\n- **`Priority` needs an ordering.** It derives `PartialEq, Eq` but not `Ord` (pm.rs:75-79), and the variant declaration order is Low → Normal → High → Urgent, so a derived `Ord` would sort ascending-by-urgency — i.e. exactly backwards from what anyone wants by default. Decide deliberately rather than deriving and hoping.\n\n**Persist the choice.** `ConfigStore::update` (pm-ui/src/config.rs:113-137) already persists `ui_scale`, `watchjump`, `author` and `recent` to ~/.pm/config.json; add a sort key the same way. Note this is a *user* preference, so it belongs in `Config`, not in pm.json5 where it would be committed and shared.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-4** asked for this in its comment (\"we need a sorting system or categorization system\"). Sorting by priority is only useful once priority is visible in the row, which is PM-4's other half — worth doing them together.\n- **PM-63** (make active filter/sort obvious) — a sort that silently persists across restarts is confusing unless the header says what it is.\n- **PM-62** (kanban) needs grouping, which is the same machinery pointed sideways; whatever `shown_tickets` grows here should be reusable there.\n\n## Open question\nShould the default change? Oldest-first is a defensible default for a tracker you read top-to-bottom, but with 55 open tickets it buries everything recent. Recommend defaulting to `updated` descending and letting the config remember otherwise.\n\n## Verify\nSwitch sort modes with a mixed set of priorities and statuses, confirm the order is correct and stable (no reshuffling on repaint), that it composes with both the status filter and the search box rather than replacing them, and that the choice survives an app restart."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 30,
      "title": "A global watchjump mode, toggled in the footer bar, where it would immediately jump to a file where it just changed, useful for working with code editor",
      "body": "",
      "status": "done",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1788474238,
      "updated": 1788495384,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788495328,
          "body": "Done (c1290eb + 7a8d267). Footer \"Watchjump\" toggle (also View → Watchjump), persisted in ~/.pm/config.json as Config.watchjump. When on, a filesystem change to any file in the git change list opens that file in the diff pane and scrolls to the first changed line (drops a caret on it). AppState::changed_in_batch picks the last qualifying path in a watcher batch; vetoes the batch if it touched .git (checkout/commit/rebase), and matching the change list already excludes gitignored paths. No forced view switch — silent while on Summary/Tickets. Has its own assets/watchjump.svg icon.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": []
    },
    {
      "id": 31,
      "title": "TextInput in ticket doesn't expand to newline and doesn't wrap, it just goes",
      "body": "",
      "status": "done",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "tickets"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "",
      "created": 1788474256,
      "updated": 1788466000,
      "anchors": [
        {
          "file": "crates/pm-ui/src/text_input.rs",
          "start_line": 1,
          "end_line": 1,
          "rev": "working"
        }
      ],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "reaganiwadha",
          "created": 1788474419,
          "body": "Follow soft-wrapping in how zed does it",
          "anchor": null
        },
        {
          "id": 2,
          "author": "reaganiwadha",
          "created": 1788461000,
          "body": "Done the Zed way: TextElement shapes with window.text_system().shape_text(text, size, runs, wrap_width, None), which returns one WrappedLine per '\\n' line, each soft-wrapped to the field width. The element measures the wrapped height and feeds it back through measured_height, so a multiline field grows with its text and the detail pane scrolls. Caret/selection/mouse all go through WrappedLine::position_for_index and closest_index_for_position so they follow wrap rows. Single-line fields pass wrap_width=None and keep horizontal scroll. Shipped with PM-7's crash fix in the same commit — needs a run to confirm it feels right.",
          "anchor": null
        },
        {
          "id": 3,
          "author": "reaganiwadha",
          "created": 1788466000,
          "body": "Closing. Multiline fields soft-wrap to width and grow with content (shape_text + measured height), caret/selection/mouse are wrap-row aware; single-line fields keep horizontal scroll.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": []
    },
    {
