    {
      "id": 50,
      "title": "Ticket linking: typed relations (blocks, parent/child, closes, relates) + hierarchy view",
      "body": "**Scope settled 2026-09-05.** Earlier draft offered three options and recommended the cheapest (linkify prose mentions). That was overruled deliberately: the goal is **semantics and hierarchy**, not just navigation. All four relation kinds are wanted.\n\n## What exists today\nNothing. `Ticket` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:165-197) has no relation field of any kind; `HistoryEvent` (:141) has no link variant.\n\nThe need is already being met by hand, badly, and it decays. Bodies across this project carry prose dependencies — \"Part of PM-55 (phase 3/5)\", \"unblocks PM-2\", \"closes PM-28\", \"Blocked on PM-71 and PM-72\". PM-55 maintains a five-phase plan as a numbered list in its body; PM-81's entire content is \"split into PM-82 → PM-83 → PM-84\". None of it is machine-readable, and it goes stale silently: **PM-78 sat at `blocked` after both of its blockers had shipped**, because the dependency existed only as English.\n\n## Model\nAdd to `Ticket`:\n\n```rust\n#[serde(default)]\npub links: Vec<Link>,\n\npub struct Link { pub kind: LinkKind, pub target: u64 }\n\npub enum LinkKind { Blocks, ParentOf, Closes, DuplicateOf, Relates }\n```\n\n**Store one canonical direction; derive the inverse.** \"PM-82 blocks PM-83\" is stored once, on PM-82; PM-83's \"blocked by\" list is computed by scanning. Storing both sides means two writers (GUI and MCP, pm-mcp/src/ops.rs:164-224) must keep them consistent forever, and any crash between the two writes leaves a dangling half-link. Lookups are already linear scans (`ticket()`/`ticket_mut()`, pm.rs:327/331) over ~90 tickets — deriving costs nothing at this scale, and if it ever does, build an index rather than denormalising.\n\n`Relates` is the one symmetric kind; either store it canonically on the lower id, or accept it being one-directional and always render both ways.\n\nNeeds: a `LinksChanged` variant on `HistoryEvent` (pm.rs:141), a `set_links` mutator following the `set_labels` template (pm.rs:496-516), the `AppState` wrapper (see PM-85, which is adding five of these already — this is a sixth), and `links` on the MCP `edit_ticket` tool.\n\n## Behaviour, per kind\n- **Blocks / blocked-by** — the one that drives behaviour. A ticket whose blockers are all `Done` should be visibly *ready*; a ticket with open blockers should say which. Add a \"ready to work on\" filter alongside the status filter (tickets_view.rs:217-269).\n- **Parent / child** — tree indentation in the ticket list and a progress rollup (\"3 of 5 done\"). Note the list is currently plain `div`s in an `overflow_y_scroll` container (tickets_view.rs:365-427) with no indentation concept — `tree_view.rs` already solves indent+chevron rendering for files and is the reference, and PM-74 is extracting exactly that pattern into fremantle. **Sequence this after PM-74** if the hierarchy view wants to reuse it.\n- **Closes** — when PM-84 goes `Done`, PM-28 should be closed too.\n- **Duplicate-of** — PM-16 may duplicate PM-28. Mostly a display concern: collapse duplicates in the list, redirect to the canonical ticket.\n\n## Do not auto-mutate status\nRecommendation, to be argued with rather than assumed: pm should *surface* consequences, not silently apply them. Auto-closing PM-28 when PM-84 lands writes a history entry nobody asked for, and auto-flipping `blocked`→`open` fights the user every time they set it deliberately. Prefer a visible affordance (\"PM-84 closed this — close PM-28 too?\") plus the ready filter. The exception worth considering is *un*blocking, since that's the failure PM-78 actually demonstrated.\n\n## Validation\n- **Cycle detection** on `ParentOf` (and arguably `Blocks`) — a parent loop hangs any tree render.\n- **Dangling targets**: there is no ticket deletion today, so links can't be orphaned yet — but `target` ids are unvalidated on write, and the MCP server will happily accept a typo. Validate on write, render unknown ids as inert.\n- Cross-project links are out of scope; ids are only unique within one pm.json5. Relevant to PM-67 (multi-project workspace) if that ever merges ticket lists.\n\n## Bootstrap from the prose that's already there\nOne-time migration worth building: scan every body for `{project.key}-{id}` mentions near the phrases already in use (\"part of\", \"closes\", \"blocked on\", \"unblocks\", \"depends on\") and offer them as real links to confirm. PM-55, PM-70..74, PM-81..84 and PM-2 would populate the graph immediately, and it turns the existing hand-maintained plans into live data instead of stranding them.\n\nSeparately, and much cheaper: **linkify bare `PM-N` mentions at render time** so they're clickable even where no typed link exists. That was option (c) of the old draft; it's still worth doing, it just isn't the whole ticket any more. It pairs with **PM-9** (markdown engine) — same inline-span pass.\n\n## Depends on\n- **PM-86** — `links` is precisely the field an older binary silently deletes on save, and losing the dependency graph is worse than losing a label. Land PM-86's guard first.\n- **PM-85** — the `AppState` mutator layer.\n- **PM-74** — if the hierarchy view reuses the extracted tree/list primitive.\n\n## Open questions\n1. Should `Closes` fire automatically, or prompt? (See above — I'd prompt.)\n2. Can a ticket have multiple parents? Recommend no; it makes the tree a DAG and the rollup ambiguous.\n3. Does the ticket list default to flat-with-indent, or a real collapsible tree? Collapsible means persisting expansion state (compare PM-39, which wants pane state in the config store).\n\n## Verify\nBuild the PM-81 → PM-82/83/84 hierarchy and the PM-55 → PM-70..74 phase chain as real links. Confirm: the tree renders with a correct rollup; PM-78's blockers show as satisfied (both `Done`) and it appears in the ready filter; a cycle is rejected; the links round-trip through `mcp__pm__get_ticket` and survive a GUI save; and a ticket with no links renders exactly as it does today.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "high",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788491393,
      "updated": 1788623445,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622586,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "",
          "new": "**A design ticket — settle the approach before writing code.**\n\n## What exists today\nNothing. `Ticket` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:165-197) has no `parent`, `children`, `blocks`, `depends_on` or `related` field, and `HistoryEvent` (:141) has no link variant.\n\nBut the *need* is already being met by hand, badly. Ticket bodies across this project are full of prose cross-references — \"Part of PM-55 (phase 3/5)\", \"unblocks PM-2\", \"closes PM-28\", \"Blocked on PM-71 and PM-72\". PM-55 maintains a five-phase plan in its body as a numbered list, and PM-81 is a tracking ticket whose entire content is \"split into PM-82 → PM-83 → PM-84\". None of it is machine-readable, none of it is clickable, and none of it stays true: PM-78 sat at `blocked` after both its blockers landed, precisely because the dependency lived only in prose.\n\n## Three options\n**(a) A typed `links: Vec<Link>` field** — `Link { kind: Blocks|BlockedBy|ParentOf|ChildOf|Relates, target: u64 }`. Most capable; enables a real hierarchy view and automatic unblocking. Costs the most: a schema addition (see PM-86), reciprocal-link maintenance, cycle detection, and a `HistoryEvent` variant. Note `ticket()`/`ticket_mut()` are linear scans (pm.rs:327/331) with no index, and there is no ticket deletion at all — so no cascade problem yet, but also no cleanup story.\n\n**(b) Lean on labels** — `Vec<String>` already exists and PM-38 wants an editor for it. Nearly free, but it can express grouping and not direction: \"PM-82 blocks PM-83\" is not something a shared label can say.\n\n**(c) Auto-detect the `PM-N` mentions already in the bodies** — parse `{project.key}-{id}` (the format `display_id()` already produces, pm.rs:336) at render time and linkify it. **No schema change at all**, works retroactively on every ticket written so far, and makes the cross-references that already exist navigable today.\n\n## Recommendation\nStart with **(c)**, then decide whether (a) is still wanted. It's a rendering change, not a data change; it delivers most of the day-to-day value (clicking \"PM-72\" to see if it landed); and it costs nothing if the answer later turns out to be (a). It also pairs naturally with **PM-9** (markdown engine) — linkification is the same pass that would find inline code and URLs, so if PM-9 lands first this is nearly free, and if it doesn't, the `TextRun` machinery for coloured spans already exists (`runs_for_line`, diff_view.rs:113-132).\n\nUntyped mentions are direction-less, which is (c)'s real ceiling: it can show \"PM-82 is mentioned here\" but never \"PM-82 must be done first\". If dependency-aware behaviour is genuinely wanted — auto-unblocking, a hierarchy view, ordering the backlog by what's ready — that's the argument for (a), and it should be made explicitly rather than drifted into.\n\n## Open questions\n- Is the goal *navigation* (click through to a referenced ticket) or *semantics* (pm knows what blocks what and acts on it)? These want different answers.\n- If (a): should links be reciprocal — does setting \"PM-83 blocked by PM-82\" write a link on PM-82 too? Storing one side is simpler; storing both keeps lookups cheap but needs both to stay consistent across two writers (GUI and MCP).\n- Epics/parents: is PM-55's numbered-phase-list-in-the-body pattern good enough, or does it want first-class support?\n\n## Verify\nDepends on the option chosen. For (c): open a ticket whose body mentions another (PM-55, PM-81 and PM-84 all do), click through, confirm it selects the right ticket — and confirm a mention of a **nonexistent** id renders as plain text rather than a dead link."
        },
        {
          "at": 1788623445,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "title_changed",
          "old": "Ticket Issue linking/issue hierarchy",
          "new": "Ticket linking: typed relations (blocks, parent/child, closes, relates) + hierarchy view"
        },
        {
          "at": 1788623445,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "**A design ticket — settle the approach before writing code.**\n\n## What exists today\nNothing. `Ticket` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:165-197) has no `parent`, `children`, `blocks`, `depends_on` or `related` field, and `HistoryEvent` (:141) has no link variant.\n\nBut the *need* is already being met by hand, badly. Ticket bodies across this project are full of prose cross-references — \"Part of PM-55 (phase 3/5)\", \"unblocks PM-2\", \"closes PM-28\", \"Blocked on PM-71 and PM-72\". PM-55 maintains a five-phase plan in its body as a numbered list, and PM-81 is a tracking ticket whose entire content is \"split into PM-82 → PM-83 → PM-84\". None of it is machine-readable, none of it is clickable, and none of it stays true: PM-78 sat at `blocked` after both its blockers landed, precisely because the dependency lived only in prose.\n\n## Three options\n**(a) A typed `links: Vec<Link>` field** — `Link { kind: Blocks|BlockedBy|ParentOf|ChildOf|Relates, target: u64 }`. Most capable; enables a real hierarchy view and automatic unblocking. Costs the most: a schema addition (see PM-86), reciprocal-link maintenance, cycle detection, and a `HistoryEvent` variant. Note `ticket()`/`ticket_mut()` are linear scans (pm.rs:327/331) with no index, and there is no ticket deletion at all — so no cascade problem yet, but also no cleanup story.\n\n**(b) Lean on labels** — `Vec<String>` already exists and PM-38 wants an editor for it. Nearly free, but it can express grouping and not direction: \"PM-82 blocks PM-83\" is not something a shared label can say.\n\n**(c) Auto-detect the `PM-N` mentions already in the bodies** — parse `{project.key}-{id}` (the format `display_id()` already produces, pm.rs:336) at render time and linkify it. **No schema change at all**, works retroactively on every ticket written so far, and makes the cross-references that already exist navigable today.\n\n## Recommendation\nStart with **(c)**, then decide whether (a) is still wanted. It's a rendering change, not a data change; it delivers most of the day-to-day value (clicking \"PM-72\" to see if it landed); and it costs nothing if the answer later turns out to be (a). It also pairs naturally with **PM-9** (markdown engine) — linkification is the same pass that would find inline code and URLs, so if PM-9 lands first this is nearly free, and if it doesn't, the `TextRun` machinery for coloured spans already exists (`runs_for_line`, diff_view.rs:113-132).\n\nUntyped mentions are direction-less, which is (c)'s real ceiling: it can show \"PM-82 is mentioned here\" but never \"PM-82 must be done first\". If dependency-aware behaviour is genuinely wanted — auto-unblocking, a hierarchy view, ordering the backlog by what's ready — that's the argument for (a), and it should be made explicitly rather than drifted into.\n\n## Open questions\n- Is the goal *navigation* (click through to a referenced ticket) or *semantics* (pm knows what blocks what and acts on it)? These want different answers.\n- If (a): should links be reciprocal — does setting \"PM-83 blocked by PM-82\" write a link on PM-82 too? Storing one side is simpler; storing both keeps lookups cheap but needs both to stay consistent across two writers (GUI and MCP).\n- Epics/parents: is PM-55's numbered-phase-list-in-the-body pattern good enough, or does it want first-class support?\n\n## Verify\nDepends on the option chosen. For (c): open a ticket whose body mentions another (PM-55, PM-81 and PM-84 all do), click through, confirm it selects the right ticket — and confirm a mention of a **nonexistent** id renders as plain text rather than a dead link.",
          "new": "**Scope settled 2026-09-05.** Earlier draft offered three options and recommended the cheapest (linkify prose mentions). That was overruled deliberately: the goal is **semantics and hierarchy**, not just navigation. All four relation kinds are wanted.\n\n## What exists today\nNothing. `Ticket` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:165-197) has no relation field of any kind; `HistoryEvent` (:141) has no link variant.\n\nThe need is already being met by hand, badly, and it decays. Bodies across this project carry prose dependencies — \"Part of PM-55 (phase 3/5)\", \"unblocks PM-2\", \"closes PM-28\", \"Blocked on PM-71 and PM-72\". PM-55 maintains a five-phase plan as a numbered list in its body; PM-81's entire content is \"split into PM-82 → PM-83 → PM-84\". None of it is machine-readable, and it goes stale silently: **PM-78 sat at `blocked` after both of its blockers had shipped**, because the dependency existed only as English.\n\n## Model\nAdd to `Ticket`:\n\n```rust\n#[serde(default)]\npub links: Vec<Link>,\n\npub struct Link { pub kind: LinkKind, pub target: u64 }\n\npub enum LinkKind { Blocks, ParentOf, Closes, DuplicateOf, Relates }\n```\n\n**Store one canonical direction; derive the inverse.** \"PM-82 blocks PM-83\" is stored once, on PM-82; PM-83's \"blocked by\" list is computed by scanning. Storing both sides means two writers (GUI and MCP, pm-mcp/src/ops.rs:164-224) must keep them consistent forever, and any crash between the two writes leaves a dangling half-link. Lookups are already linear scans (`ticket()`/`ticket_mut()`, pm.rs:327/331) over ~90 tickets — deriving costs nothing at this scale, and if it ever does, build an index rather than denormalising.\n\n`Relates` is the one symmetric kind; either store it canonically on the lower id, or accept it being one-directional and always render both ways.\n\nNeeds: a `LinksChanged` variant on `HistoryEvent` (pm.rs:141), a `set_links` mutator following the `set_labels` template (pm.rs:496-516), the `AppState` wrapper (see PM-85, which is adding five of these already — this is a sixth), and `links` on the MCP `edit_ticket` tool.\n\n## Behaviour, per kind\n- **Blocks / blocked-by** — the one that drives behaviour. A ticket whose blockers are all `Done` should be visibly *ready*; a ticket with open blockers should say which. Add a \"ready to work on\" filter alongside the status filter (tickets_view.rs:217-269).\n- **Parent / child** — tree indentation in the ticket list and a progress rollup (\"3 of 5 done\"). Note the list is currently plain `div`s in an `overflow_y_scroll` container (tickets_view.rs:365-427) with no indentation concept — `tree_view.rs` already solves indent+chevron rendering for files and is the reference, and PM-74 is extracting exactly that pattern into fremantle. **Sequence this after PM-74** if the hierarchy view wants to reuse it.\n- **Closes** — when PM-84 goes `Done`, PM-28 should be closed too.\n- **Duplicate-of** — PM-16 may duplicate PM-28. Mostly a display concern: collapse duplicates in the list, redirect to the canonical ticket.\n\n## Do not auto-mutate status\nRecommendation, to be argued with rather than assumed: pm should *surface* consequences, not silently apply them. Auto-closing PM-28 when PM-84 lands writes a history entry nobody asked for, and auto-flipping `blocked`→`open` fights the user every time they set it deliberately. Prefer a visible affordance (\"PM-84 closed this — close PM-28 too?\") plus the ready filter. The exception worth considering is *un*blocking, since that's the failure PM-78 actually demonstrated.\n\n## Validation\n- **Cycle detection** on `ParentOf` (and arguably `Blocks`) — a parent loop hangs any tree render.\n- **Dangling targets**: there is no ticket deletion today, so links can't be orphaned yet — but `target` ids are unvalidated on write, and the MCP server will happily accept a typo. Validate on write, render unknown ids as inert.\n- Cross-project links are out of scope; ids are only unique within one pm.json5. Relevant to PM-67 (multi-project workspace) if that ever merges ticket lists.\n\n## Bootstrap from the prose that's already there\nOne-time migration worth building: scan every body for `{project.key}-{id}` mentions near the phrases already in use (\"part of\", \"closes\", \"blocked on\", \"unblocks\", \"depends on\") and offer them as real links to confirm. PM-55, PM-70..74, PM-81..84 and PM-2 would populate the graph immediately, and it turns the existing hand-maintained plans into live data instead of stranding them.\n\nSeparately, and much cheaper: **linkify bare `PM-N` mentions at render time** so they're clickable even where no typed link exists. That was option (c) of the old draft; it's still worth doing, it just isn't the whole ticket any more. It pairs with **PM-9** (markdown engine) — same inline-span pass.\n\n## Depends on\n- **PM-86** — `links` is precisely the field an older binary silently deletes on save, and losing the dependency graph is worse than losing a label. Land PM-86's guard first.\n- **PM-85** — the `AppState` mutator layer.\n- **PM-74** — if the hierarchy view reuses the extracted tree/list primitive.\n\n## Open questions\n1. Should `Closes` fire automatically, or prompt? (See above — I'd prompt.)\n2. Can a ticket have multiple parents? Recommend no; it makes the tree a DAG and the rollup ambiguous.\n3. Does the ticket list default to flat-with-indent, or a real collapsible tree? Collapsible means persisting expansion state (compare PM-39, which wants pane state in the config store).\n\n## Verify\nBuild the PM-81 → PM-82/83/84 hierarchy and the PM-55 → PM-70..74 phase chain as real links. Confirm: the tree renders with a correct rollup; PM-78's blockers show as satisfied (both `Done`) and it appears in the ready filter; a cycle is rejected; the links round-trip through `mcp__pm__get_ticket` and survive a GUI save; and a ticket with no links renders exactly as it does today."
        },
        {
          "at": 1788623445,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "priority_changed",
          "old": "normal",
          "new": "high"
        }
      ]
