      "id": 60,
      "title": "pm setup is still very flaky on powershell",
      "body": "",
      "status": "in_progress",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788584048,
      "updated": 1788622354,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788622354,
          "body": "Moved to in_progress — there is an uncommitted fix sitting in the working tree.\n\nThis is the **second** attempt at the same bug. Commit c04f5bf already added one `FlushConsoleInputBuffer` before the y/n read (`confirm`, crates/pm-core/src/setup.rs:121). The uncommitted diff (+11 lines at setup.rs:130) makes it flush → `sleep(150ms)` → flush → read, because under ConPTY (Windows Terminal) pasted input is translated from VT sequences into console input records *asynchronously*, so a stray Enter from the `irm | iex` paste can still land after the first flush.\n\nWorth being honest in the ticket: that is a timing race, not a fix — a slow enough machine still loses. Two things that would close it properly rather than narrow the window:\n\n- `confirm` (setup.rs:157) falls through to the default on *any* unrecognised answer, including a bare Enter — so the stray-Enter bug and a deliberate \"just press Enter\" are indistinguishable. Requiring an explicit y/n (re-prompting on empty) would make the failure visible instead of silent.\n- The trigger is `install.ps1:69-74` chaining straight into `& $target --setup` inside the piped-to-`iex` session. A `-NoSetup` switch on the installer, or having the installer pass `--yes`, sidesteps the console-input race entirely.\n\nAlso note `confirm` silently takes the default when stdin is not a terminal (setup.rs:123).",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622334,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "status_changed",
          "old": "open",
          "new": "in_progress"
        },
        {
          "at": 1788622354,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "commented",
          "comment_id": 1
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 61,
      "title": "pm installer script need linux/macos",
      "body": "",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788584061,
      "updated": 1788623496,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788623496,
          "body": "Cross-reference: **PM-88** (build variants) turns up a bug in `install.ps1` that belongs to the installer work here too.\n\n`install.ps1:37` is `Get-Process pm -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Stop-Process -Force` — it kills every process named `pm` by name, with no path check. That means installing or updating silently kills the running pm MCP server, any other pm window, and (once PM-88 lands) a debug build. It should match on the executable path it's about to overwrite, not on the process name.\n\nWhatever the linux/macOS installer ends up doing, it shouldn't inherit that pattern.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788623496,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "commented",
          "comment_id": 1
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 62,
      "title": "Kanban view of tickets",
      "body": "A board view of tickets: one column per status, tickets as cards, drag a card to change its status.\n\n## What exists today\n- `Status::ALL` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:59) gives the five columns for free: Open, In Progress, Blocked, Done, Won't Fix. `Status::color()` (:49) and `label()` (:39) give the headers.\n- `AppState::set_ticket_status` (state.rs:252) already exists and already records a `StatusChanged` history event — so the drop action is **already wired**, unusually for this backlog. `status_button` (tickets_view.rs:549-601) is the existing caller.\n- `Pm::view` is a three-value enum (app.rs:70-76) — `Summary | Files | Tickets` — cycled by `ctrl-tab` (`cycle_view`, :504-513, `ORDER` const) and bound to `ctrl-1/2/3` (src/main.rs:83-87), with a segmented switcher in title_bar.rs:90-147.\n\n## The real design question: is this a fourth top-level view, or a mode within Tickets?\nA fourth `View` variant means touching the enum, `ORDER`, the switcher, the keybindings and the window title (app.rs:404). A **layout toggle inside the Tickets view** — list ⇄ board, sharing `shown_tickets` and `selected_ticket` — is much less invasive and keeps the filter/search/sort header working across both. Recommend the toggle.\n\n## In scope\n1. A board layout: five columns, cards from `shown_tickets` (tickets_view.rs:274-286) grouped by status. Reusing `shown_tickets` means the status filter, PM-75's search and PM-29's sort all keep working — a board that ignores the active filters would be a separate, confusing surface.\n2. Drag-and-drop between columns → `set_ticket_status`. gpui drag handling is already used in this app for the sidebar and diff-split resize (`on_drag_move` → `route_sidebar_drag`, tickets_view.rs:98; `diff_split_drag`, diff_view.rs:942-963) — same primitives, different payload.\n3. Card content. Whatever PM-4 (priority marker), PM-38 (labels) and PM-66 (author) settle on for the list row applies here too — decide once.\n\n## Depends on / related\n- **PM-29** (sort) — a board needs a within-column order, which is the same machinery. Do PM-29 first.\n- **PM-4** — priority is the main thing you scan a board for; markers matter more here than in the list.\n- Note the status filter defaults to hiding Done and Wontfix (app.rs:290-293), which would render two of five columns permanently empty. The board probably wants to override that default, or PM-63's filter indicator needs to explain it.\n\n## Out of scope\nManual ordering *within* a column. There is no `order`/`rank` field on `Ticket` (pm.rs:165-197) and file order is creation order (`create_ticket` pushes, :378), so drag-to-reorder needs a schema change — see PM-86 before adding one. Columns should sort by an existing key instead.\n\n## Open question\nFive columns is a lot for a sidebar-width pane, and Won't Fix is arguably not a board column at all. Worth considering collapsing Done/Wontfix into one terminal column, or hiding them behind the filter.\n\n## Verify\nDrag a ticket between columns; confirm the status persists to .pm/pm.json5, a `StatusChanged` history entry is recorded with the right author, and the list view reflects it immediately. Check the board honours the active status filter and search rather than showing everything.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788584289,
      "updated": 1788622644,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622644,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "",
          "new": "A board view of tickets: one column per status, tickets as cards, drag a card to change its status.\n\n## What exists today\n- `Status::ALL` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:59) gives the five columns for free: Open, In Progress, Blocked, Done, Won't Fix. `Status::color()` (:49) and `label()` (:39) give the headers.\n- `AppState::set_ticket_status` (state.rs:252) already exists and already records a `StatusChanged` history event — so the drop action is **already wired**, unusually for this backlog. `status_button` (tickets_view.rs:549-601) is the existing caller.\n- `Pm::view` is a three-value enum (app.rs:70-76) — `Summary | Files | Tickets` — cycled by `ctrl-tab` (`cycle_view`, :504-513, `ORDER` const) and bound to `ctrl-1/2/3` (src/main.rs:83-87), with a segmented switcher in title_bar.rs:90-147.\n\n## The real design question: is this a fourth top-level view, or a mode within Tickets?\nA fourth `View` variant means touching the enum, `ORDER`, the switcher, the keybindings and the window title (app.rs:404). A **layout toggle inside the Tickets view** — list ⇄ board, sharing `shown_tickets` and `selected_ticket` — is much less invasive and keeps the filter/search/sort header working across both. Recommend the toggle.\n\n## In scope\n1. A board layout: five columns, cards from `shown_tickets` (tickets_view.rs:274-286) grouped by status. Reusing `shown_tickets` means the status filter, PM-75's search and PM-29's sort all keep working — a board that ignores the active filters would be a separate, confusing surface.\n2. Drag-and-drop between columns → `set_ticket_status`. gpui drag handling is already used in this app for the sidebar and diff-split resize (`on_drag_move` → `route_sidebar_drag`, tickets_view.rs:98; `diff_split_drag`, diff_view.rs:942-963) — same primitives, different payload.\n3. Card content. Whatever PM-4 (priority marker), PM-38 (labels) and PM-66 (author) settle on for the list row applies here too — decide once.\n\n## Depends on / related\n- **PM-29** (sort) — a board needs a within-column order, which is the same machinery. Do PM-29 first.\n- **PM-4** — priority is the main thing you scan a board for; markers matter more here than in the list.\n- Note the status filter defaults to hiding Done and Wontfix (app.rs:290-293), which would render two of five columns permanently empty. The board probably wants to override that default, or PM-63's filter indicator needs to explain it.\n\n## Out of scope\nManual ordering *within* a column. There is no `order`/`rank` field on `Ticket` (pm.rs:165-197) and file order is creation order (`create_ticket` pushes, :378), so drag-to-reorder needs a schema change — see PM-86 before adding one. Columns should sort by an existing key instead.\n\n## Open question\nFive columns is a lot for a sidebar-width pane, and Won't Fix is arguably not a board column at all. Worth considering collapsing Done/Wontfix into one terminal column, or hiding them behind the filter.\n\n## Verify\nDrag a ticket between columns; confirm the status persists to .pm/pm.json5, a `StatusChanged` history entry is recorded with the right author, and the list view reflects it immediately. Check the board honours the active status filter and search rather than showing everything."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 63,
      "title": "A UI More obvious about what filters is being selected",
      "body": "## What exists today\nThe list header (tickets_view.rs:319-363) carries three separate, uncoordinated pieces of filter state:\n\n1. **Status filter** — `filter_button` (:217-269), labelled with the hardcoded format string `\"Filter ({}/5) ▾\"` (:244-247). It reports *how many* statuses are checked and never *which*. `\"Filter (3/5)\"` is the default (Open/InProgress/Blocked — Done and Wontfix hidden, app.rs:290-293), and there is no way to tell from the header that closed tickets are being hidden. That is the actual bug: someone looks for a ticket they know exists, doesn't find it, and has to open a popover to discover why.\n2. **Search** (PM-75) — `search_toggle_button` (:184-214). Collapsed by default, so when the box is hidden the query is invisible too. Closing the toggle resets the input, but while collapsed-with-text there's no indicator.\n3. **The result count** — the header title already shows `\"Tickets (n)\"` or `\"Tickets (n of m)\"` (:313-317), which is the one honest signal that something is being hidden, but it doesn't say what.\n\nBoth filters compose in `shown_tickets` (:274-286), and PM-38 (label filter) and PM-29 (sort) each want to add a fourth and fifth dimension to the same header.\n\n## In scope\nMake the active filter state readable **without opening anything**. The concrete minimum:\n- Name the active statuses instead of counting them — `\"Open, In Progress, Blocked\"`, elided to `\"3 statuses\"` when it doesn't fit. Or invert it: say what's *hidden*, since that's the surprising part.\n- Show the search query when the box is collapsed but non-empty, and make it clearable in one click.\n- Give the whole header a visible \"filtered\" state, so a narrowed list never looks like a complete one.\n\n## Design note\nWorth deciding whether these stay as separate popovers or collapse into one filter strip / chip row (each active filter as a dismissable chip — `chip()` already exists at :24-32). Given PM-29 and PM-38 are both queuing up to add to this header, settling the pattern here is the point of the ticket; adding a fourth independent dropdown makes it worse.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-29** (sort) — a persisted sort order is subject to exactly the same \"why does the list look like this\" problem, so it belongs in whatever indicator this ticket builds.\n- **PM-38** (label filter) — should land after or with this, not before.\n- **PM-75** shipped the search; this is its follow-up.\n\n## Verify\nWith a non-default status set, a search query, and the search box collapsed, the header should make all of it plain at a glance — and the count in the title should reconcile with what's on screen.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788584307,
      "updated": 1788622543,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622543,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "",
          "new": "## What exists today\nThe list header (tickets_view.rs:319-363) carries three separate, uncoordinated pieces of filter state:\n\n1. **Status filter** — `filter_button` (:217-269), labelled with the hardcoded format string `\"Filter ({}/5) ▾\"` (:244-247). It reports *how many* statuses are checked and never *which*. `\"Filter (3/5)\"` is the default (Open/InProgress/Blocked — Done and Wontfix hidden, app.rs:290-293), and there is no way to tell from the header that closed tickets are being hidden. That is the actual bug: someone looks for a ticket they know exists, doesn't find it, and has to open a popover to discover why.\n2. **Search** (PM-75) — `search_toggle_button` (:184-214). Collapsed by default, so when the box is hidden the query is invisible too. Closing the toggle resets the input, but while collapsed-with-text there's no indicator.\n3. **The result count** — the header title already shows `\"Tickets (n)\"` or `\"Tickets (n of m)\"` (:313-317), which is the one honest signal that something is being hidden, but it doesn't say what.\n\nBoth filters compose in `shown_tickets` (:274-286), and PM-38 (label filter) and PM-29 (sort) each want to add a fourth and fifth dimension to the same header.\n\n## In scope\nMake the active filter state readable **without opening anything**. The concrete minimum:\n- Name the active statuses instead of counting them — `\"Open, In Progress, Blocked\"`, elided to `\"3 statuses\"` when it doesn't fit. Or invert it: say what's *hidden*, since that's the surprising part.\n- Show the search query when the box is collapsed but non-empty, and make it clearable in one click.\n- Give the whole header a visible \"filtered\" state, so a narrowed list never looks like a complete one.\n\n## Design note\nWorth deciding whether these stay as separate popovers or collapse into one filter strip / chip row (each active filter as a dismissable chip — `chip()` already exists at :24-32). Given PM-29 and PM-38 are both queuing up to add to this header, settling the pattern here is the point of the ticket; adding a fourth independent dropdown makes it worse.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-29** (sort) — a persisted sort order is subject to exactly the same \"why does the list look like this\" problem, so it belongs in whatever indicator this ticket builds.\n- **PM-38** (label filter) — should land after or with this, not before.\n- **PM-75** shipped the search; this is its follow-up.\n\n## Verify\nWith a non-default status set, a search query, and the search box collapsed, the header should make all of it plain at a glance — and the count in the title should reconcile with what's on screen."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 64,
      "title": "Pressing arrow up on a text input should kind of act like the home button? And arrow down should act like end button. Need it to behave like how browsers do",
      "body": "Concrete and unblocked — no dependency on the PM-81 focus work.\n\n## The behaviour\nIn a **single-line** text input, Up should behave as Home and Down as End, matching browsers and every native single-line field. There is no line above or below in a single-line input, so the current behaviour is either nothing or something surprising.\n\n**Multiline inputs keep real line motion** — Up/Down must stay Up/Down there. This is the whole reason it's a per-context change rather than a global remap.\n\n## Where the code is\n- Actions `Up`, `Down`, `Home`, `End` are declared in `actions!(pm_text_input, [...])`, crates/fremantle/src/text_input.rs:41-74, with handlers wired via `.on_action(cx.listener(..))` at :764-790.\n- The `multiline` flag is a public field on `TextInput` (:91), set by the `single()` / `multi()` constructors (:114, :118).\n- Key bindings live in the **consumer**, not fremantle: `bind_text_input_keys`, src/main.rs:145-178, already scoped to the three contexts `TextInput` / `TextInputSingleLine` / `TextInputMultiLine`, which `TextInput::render` declares at text_input.rs:746-750.\n\nSo the context machinery this needs already exists. The change is binding `up`/`down` to `Home`/`End` under `TextInputSingleLine` specifically, leaving the `TextInputMultiLine` bindings alone.\n\n## Also decide: the selecting variants\nThe action list includes `SelectUp`/`SelectDown` and `SelectHome`/`SelectEnd` (:41-74). Shift+Up in a single-line field should presumably select to the start, by the same argument. Handle both or neither — half of it is worse than not doing it.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-87** (fremantle pm-isms) renames the `pm_text_input` action namespace, which touches the same `bind_text_input_keys` block. Trivial either way, but expect a conflict if both are in flight.\n- **PM-84** also touches the `TextInputSingleLine` context for Tab.\n\n## Verify\nIn the ticket search box and the new-ticket title (both `TextInput::single`, app.rs:210/219): Up jumps to the start, Down to the end. In the description and comment boxes (`TextInput::multi`, app.rs:216-218): Up/Down still move between lines. Check Shift+Up/Down matches whatever was decided for the selecting variants.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788584357,
      "updated": 1788622735,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622735,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "",
          "new": "Concrete and unblocked — no dependency on the PM-81 focus work.\n\n## The behaviour\nIn a **single-line** text input, Up should behave as Home and Down as End, matching browsers and every native single-line field. There is no line above or below in a single-line input, so the current behaviour is either nothing or something surprising.\n\n**Multiline inputs keep real line motion** — Up/Down must stay Up/Down there. This is the whole reason it's a per-context change rather than a global remap.\n\n## Where the code is\n- Actions `Up`, `Down`, `Home`, `End` are declared in `actions!(pm_text_input, [...])`, crates/fremantle/src/text_input.rs:41-74, with handlers wired via `.on_action(cx.listener(..))` at :764-790.\n- The `multiline` flag is a public field on `TextInput` (:91), set by the `single()` / `multi()` constructors (:114, :118).\n- Key bindings live in the **consumer**, not fremantle: `bind_text_input_keys`, src/main.rs:145-178, already scoped to the three contexts `TextInput` / `TextInputSingleLine` / `TextInputMultiLine`, which `TextInput::render` declares at text_input.rs:746-750.\n\nSo the context machinery this needs already exists. The change is binding `up`/`down` to `Home`/`End` under `TextInputSingleLine` specifically, leaving the `TextInputMultiLine` bindings alone.\n\n## Also decide: the selecting variants\nThe action list includes `SelectUp`/`SelectDown` and `SelectHome`/`SelectEnd` (:41-74). Shift+Up in a single-line field should presumably select to the start, by the same argument. Handle both or neither — half of it is worse than not doing it.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-87** (fremantle pm-isms) renames the `pm_text_input` action namespace, which touches the same `bind_text_input_keys` block. Trivial either way, but expect a conflict if both are in flight.\n- **PM-84** also touches the `TextInputSingleLine` context for Tab.\n\n## Verify\nIn the ticket search box and the new-ticket title (both `TextInput::single`, app.rs:210/219): Up jumps to the start, Down to the end. In the description and comment boxes (`TextInput::multi`, app.rs:216-218): Up/Down still move between lines. Check Shift+Up/Down matches whatever was decided for the selecting variants."
        }
      ]
    },
