    {
      "id": 66,
      "title": "In the ticket list, we should be able to see who made that issue immediately.",
      "body": "Small, self-contained, no dependencies — a good starter ticket.\n\n## What exists today\nThe author is already stored and already displayed, just not in the list. `Ticket.author` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:182) is rendered in the **detail** meta row as `by {author}`, but only when non-blank — tickets_view.rs:630-637. List rows (:378-427) show: status dot, `display_id`, `\"{status} · {priority}\"`, title. No author.\n\n## In scope\nAdd the author to the list row. Everything needed is already resolved:\n- Blank authors are common — every ticket created before PM-15 has `author: \"\"`. The comment renderer already handles this by falling back to `\"someone\"` (:710-738); match that, or omit the field entirely for blanks rather than rendering a stray `by`.\n- `rel_time()` (history_view.rs:59) is already used by this file if a timestamp goes alongside.\n\n## The real constraint: the row is getting crowded\nThree open tickets all want to add something to the same row — this one (author), PM-4 (a priority marker), PM-38 (labels). Row height is `list_row_h` from the theme and the sidebar is user-resizable from `sidebar_min` (fremantle/src/theme.rs:37-62), so there's a real width budget. Worth deciding the row's final layout **once**, across all three, instead of three separate squeezes. A second dim line under the title is the obvious place — `\"{status} · {priority}\"` already occupies one, so author could join it.\n\n## Open question\nAuthor, or assignee, or both? `assignee: Option<String>` (pm.rs:179) also exists and is also detail-only (:623-629). \"Who made that issue\" is what this ticket asks for, but in practice \"who is on it\" is often what you're scanning for. Both is probably one field too many for a list row.\n\n## Verify\nOpen the Tickets view, confirm authors show on rows including the fallback for the blank-author tickets that predate PM-15, and check the row still reads cleanly with the sidebar dragged to its minimum width.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788584427,
      "updated": 1788622508,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622508,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "",
          "new": "Small, self-contained, no dependencies — a good starter ticket.\n\n## What exists today\nThe author is already stored and already displayed, just not in the list. `Ticket.author` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:182) is rendered in the **detail** meta row as `by {author}`, but only when non-blank — tickets_view.rs:630-637. List rows (:378-427) show: status dot, `display_id`, `\"{status} · {priority}\"`, title. No author.\n\n## In scope\nAdd the author to the list row. Everything needed is already resolved:\n- Blank authors are common — every ticket created before PM-15 has `author: \"\"`. The comment renderer already handles this by falling back to `\"someone\"` (:710-738); match that, or omit the field entirely for blanks rather than rendering a stray `by`.\n- `rel_time()` (history_view.rs:59) is already used by this file if a timestamp goes alongside.\n\n## The real constraint: the row is getting crowded\nThree open tickets all want to add something to the same row — this one (author), PM-4 (a priority marker), PM-38 (labels). Row height is `list_row_h` from the theme and the sidebar is user-resizable from `sidebar_min` (fremantle/src/theme.rs:37-62), so there's a real width budget. Worth deciding the row's final layout **once**, across all three, instead of three separate squeezes. A second dim line under the title is the obvious place — `\"{status} · {priority}\"` already occupies one, so author could join it.\n\n## Open question\nAuthor, or assignee, or both? `assignee: Option<String>` (pm.rs:179) also exists and is also detail-only (:623-629). \"Who made that issue\" is what this ticket asks for, but in practice \"who is on it\" is often what you're scanning for. Both is probably one field too many for a list row.\n\n## Verify\nOpen the Tickets view, confirm authors show on rows including the fallback for the blank-author tickets that predate PM-15, and check the row still reads cleanly with the sidebar dragged to its minimum width."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
