    {
      "id": 76,
      "title": "MCP and Watermarking needs to be obvious to coding agents so that coding agents use it and warns the user and utilizes pm properly when it's a pm project",
      "body": "Umbrella ticket. Body written 2026-09-05 in response to its own \"Needs Claude Code input\" — the concrete pieces are split into subtickets below; what remains here is the **discoverability** half, which nothing else covers.\n\n## The problem, split in two\n**(a) Capability** — can an agent do the right thing with pm? Mostly a matter of missing tools; see the subtickets.\n\n**(b) Discoverability** — does an agent *know* pm is here and that it should care? This is the harder half and the one this ticket keeps.\n\n## Nothing currently signals \"this is a pm project\"\nVerified by grep across the repo: pm writes **no** `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.cursorrules`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, project-level `.mcp.json`, README badge, or `.gitignore` entry. `PmData::save_in` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:310-325) creates `.pm/` and writes exactly one file into it: `pm.json5`.\n\nSo the total set of signals available to an agent today is:\n1. The MCP `instructions` string (crates/pm-mcp/src/lib.rs:194-200) — **global and static**, identical for every project, and only visible if the pm server is already registered.\n2. Per-tool descriptions (lib.rs:126-186).\n3. `list_projects` — useful only to an agent that already knows to ask.\n4. The `~/.claude.json` registration written by `pm --setup` (setup.rs:385-421) — Claude Code only, user scope, **Windows only**.\n\nAn agent working in a checkout of this repo with no pm MCP server registered has no way to discover that 95 tickets of context exist. It will do the work and file nothing.\n\n## What \"watermarking\" could mean\nDeliberately not deciding here — this wants a design pass:\n- A project-level hint file (`AGENTS.md`, or a section appended to an existing `CLAUDE.md`) written when a project first becomes a pm project. **The natural hook already exists**: the store-location dialog (app.rs:1488-1530, applied :478-499) is the exact moment pm decides a repo is a pm project, and today it writes nothing but config state.\n- A project-level `.mcp.json` so the server registers itself for anyone who opens the repo.\n- Something in `.pm/` itself — a README next to `pm.json5` explaining what it is to whoever finds it.\n\nOpen question worth taking seriously: **writing files into someone's repo is intrusive**, and doubly so if it's committed and affects their teammates. Opt-in at store-choice time seems right; silently generating an `AGENTS.md` does not. There's also a real question of whether pm should *edit* an existing `CLAUDE.md` it didn't write.\n\n## \"Warns the user\" (from the title)\nUnresolved and worth pinning down: warn about what, exactly? Candidates — a coding agent made changes without touching any ticket; a ticket's referenced code has changed since it was written (compare PM-89 on anchor staleness); a commit message mentions `PM-N` but that ticket is still open. The last one is real and demonstrated: PM-72, PM-75, PM-80 and PM-22 all shipped with the ticket id in the commit message and all stayed `open`. pm already has the git layer to detect it (`Repo::log`, git.rs:229).\n\n## Subtickets\n- **PM-90** — rewrite the MCP instructions to say *when* to use pm. Cheapest, highest leverage.\n- **PM-91** — a search/query tool (agents can't check for existing tickets today).\n- **PM-92** — bulk body reads in `list_tickets`, and fix its misleading description.\n- **PM-93** — dedupe warning on `create_ticket`.\n- **PM-94** — batch edits.\n- **PM-95** — agent-vs-human provenance.\n- **PM-89** — anchors as a first-class feature; the MCP surface can't write them at all, which is the single largest capability gap.\n\n## Also relevant\n- **PM-88** — once two binaries exist, \"which pm is the agent driving\" becomes ambiguous; `open_project`'s binary resolution (ops.rs:237-256) and the MCP registration both need to say which variant they mean.\n- `crates/pm-mcp/README.md:55` recommends registering from source as `cargo run -q -p pm -- --mcp`, which makes the MCP server hold cargo's build lock on `target/` — an agent whose MCP server is cargo deadlocks against its own builds. That advice should be corrected or warned about.\n\n## Verify\nThe end state is behavioural, not mechanical: a fresh agent session opened on a pm project it has never seen should discover the ticket store on its own, check it before starting work, and close what it finishes — without the user mentioning pm.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788591064,
      "updated": 1788624664,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788623501,
          "body": "Cross-reference from **PM-88** (build variants), since it touches the same \"which pm is the agent talking to\" question.\n\nTwo findings relevant here:\n\n1. `crates/pm-mcp/README.md:55` recommends registering the server from source as `{\"command\": \"cargo\", \"args\": [\"run\", \"-q\", \"-p\", \"pm\", \"--\", \"--mcp\"]}`. That makes the MCP server hold cargo's build lock on `target/`, so any concurrent `cargo run`/`cargo check` blocks on \"waiting for file lock on build directory\" until the server exits. If the agent's own MCP server is cargo, the agent building the project deadlocks against itself. This documented advice should probably carry a warning or be replaced.\n\n2. `open_project` (crates/pm-mcp/src/ops.rs:237-256) resolves the GUI via `$PM_BIN` → sibling `pm`/`pm.exe` → `pm` on PATH. Once PM-88 ships a second binary that resolution is ambiguous, and an agent could launch the wrong variant.\n\nBoth feed the broader point of this ticket: an agent currently has no reliable way to tell *which* pm it is driving, or that a project is a pm project at all. The MCP `instructions` string (crates/pm-mcp/src/lib.rs:194-200) is the only global signal, and it says nothing about when to use pm.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788623501,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "commented",
          "comment_id": 1
        },
        {
          "at": 1788624664,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "Needs Claude Code input",
          "new": "Umbrella ticket. Body written 2026-09-05 in response to its own \"Needs Claude Code input\" — the concrete pieces are split into subtickets below; what remains here is the **discoverability** half, which nothing else covers.\n\n## The problem, split in two\n**(a) Capability** — can an agent do the right thing with pm? Mostly a matter of missing tools; see the subtickets.\n\n**(b) Discoverability** — does an agent *know* pm is here and that it should care? This is the harder half and the one this ticket keeps.\n\n## Nothing currently signals \"this is a pm project\"\nVerified by grep across the repo: pm writes **no** `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.cursorrules`, `.github/copilot-instructions.md`, project-level `.mcp.json`, README badge, or `.gitignore` entry. `PmData::save_in` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:310-325) creates `.pm/` and writes exactly one file into it: `pm.json5`.\n\nSo the total set of signals available to an agent today is:\n1. The MCP `instructions` string (crates/pm-mcp/src/lib.rs:194-200) — **global and static**, identical for every project, and only visible if the pm server is already registered.\n2. Per-tool descriptions (lib.rs:126-186).\n3. `list_projects` — useful only to an agent that already knows to ask.\n4. The `~/.claude.json` registration written by `pm --setup` (setup.rs:385-421) — Claude Code only, user scope, **Windows only**.\n\nAn agent working in a checkout of this repo with no pm MCP server registered has no way to discover that 95 tickets of context exist. It will do the work and file nothing.\n\n## What \"watermarking\" could mean\nDeliberately not deciding here — this wants a design pass:\n- A project-level hint file (`AGENTS.md`, or a section appended to an existing `CLAUDE.md`) written when a project first becomes a pm project. **The natural hook already exists**: the store-location dialog (app.rs:1488-1530, applied :478-499) is the exact moment pm decides a repo is a pm project, and today it writes nothing but config state.\n- A project-level `.mcp.json` so the server registers itself for anyone who opens the repo.\n- Something in `.pm/` itself — a README next to `pm.json5` explaining what it is to whoever finds it.\n\nOpen question worth taking seriously: **writing files into someone's repo is intrusive**, and doubly so if it's committed and affects their teammates. Opt-in at store-choice time seems right; silently generating an `AGENTS.md` does not. There's also a real question of whether pm should *edit* an existing `CLAUDE.md` it didn't write.\n\n## \"Warns the user\" (from the title)\nUnresolved and worth pinning down: warn about what, exactly? Candidates — a coding agent made changes without touching any ticket; a ticket's referenced code has changed since it was written (compare PM-89 on anchor staleness); a commit message mentions `PM-N` but that ticket is still open. The last one is real and demonstrated: PM-72, PM-75, PM-80 and PM-22 all shipped with the ticket id in the commit message and all stayed `open`. pm already has the git layer to detect it (`Repo::log`, git.rs:229).\n\n## Subtickets\n- **PM-90** — rewrite the MCP instructions to say *when* to use pm. Cheapest, highest leverage.\n- **PM-91** — a search/query tool (agents can't check for existing tickets today).\n- **PM-92** — bulk body reads in `list_tickets`, and fix its misleading description.\n- **PM-93** — dedupe warning on `create_ticket`.\n- **PM-94** — batch edits.\n- **PM-95** — agent-vs-human provenance.\n- **PM-89** — anchors as a first-class feature; the MCP surface can't write them at all, which is the single largest capability gap.\n\n## Also relevant\n- **PM-88** — once two binaries exist, \"which pm is the agent driving\" becomes ambiguous; `open_project`'s binary resolution (ops.rs:237-256) and the MCP registration both need to say which variant they mean.\n- `crates/pm-mcp/README.md:55` recommends registering from source as `cargo run -q -p pm -- --mcp`, which makes the MCP server hold cargo's build lock on `target/` — an agent whose MCP server is cargo deadlocks against its own builds. That advice should be corrected or warned about.\n\n## Verify\nThe end state is behavioural, not mechanical: a fresh agent session opened on a pm project it has never seen should discover the ticket store on its own, check it before starting work, and close what it finishes — without the user mentioning pm."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 77,
      "title": "fremantle: comments should remove assumptions that it's being used for pm, it should be generic ui primitives",
      "body": "Audited both prose and API surface. Fixed:\n\n- `lib.rs` crate doc said \"`pm-ui` is fremantle's first consumer; see PM-70..PM-74\" — removed, describes fremantle standalone now.\n- `scroll.rs` module doc said \"shared by the diff body and the file list\" (pm-ui domain language) — reworded to \"shared by any scrollable region\".\n\n`decorations.rs`'s doc only references Linux/Zed (attribution, not pm) — no change needed. API surface (`DecorationStyle`, `client_side_decorations`, `ScrollState`, `Axis`, `BarInfo`, `ScrollDrag`) was already generic — colors/metrics are caller-supplied params, no pm types or naming leak in. No renames needed.",
      "status": "done",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "fremantle"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788593030,
      "updated": 1788593658,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788593658,
          "author": "reaganiwadha",
          "kind": "status_changed",
          "old": "open",
          "new": "done"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 78,
      "title": "a fremantle binary with a list and layout switcher to see all available components",
      "body": "A showcase/gallery app: a sidebar list of fremantle's components, a main panel showing whichever one is selected. Storybook-style, for browsing what fremantle offers.\n\nScoping decisions:\n\n- **Structure**: `crates/fremantle/examples/gallery.rs`, run via `cargo run -p fremantle --example gallery`. No new workspace member, no `[[bin]]` — an example living next to what it showcases.\n- **Bootstrap**: plain `gpui::App::new()` + `cx.open_window(..)`, not pm's `gpui_platform::application()` wrapper — that wrapper only exists to pick Linux windowing backends (`wayland`/`x11` features) for the shipped `pm` binary; a dev-only example doesn't need that ceremony. If Linux launch ever needs backend selection, add `gpui_platform` as a dev-dependency then — not a blocker now.\n- **Shape**: mirror pm-ui's list+detail split visually (`tickets_view.rs`'s sidebar-list + detail-panel pattern) for familiarity — styling reference only; fremantle cannot depend on pm-ui (backwards dependency).\n\n**Blocked on PM-71 (Theme) and PM-72 (TextInput)**: right now fremantle only has `scroll` (pure math, nothing visual to show) and `decorations` (wraps a whole window, not a list-browsable item) — nothing substantive to switch between yet. Revisit once those land.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "fremantle"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788593081,
      "updated": 1788622388,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788622388,
          "body": "Unblocked — both blockers have landed. PM-71 (Theme, f005df5) and PM-72 (TextInput, 3925d0c) are done, so fremantle now has two genuinely showable components plus `decorations` and `scroll`.\n\nConfirming the scoping decisions in the body still hold against the current tree: `crates/fremantle/Cargo.toml` has no `[[bin]]` and no `examples/` dir yet, and its only dependency is `gpui` — so `examples/gallery.rs` run via `cargo run -p fremantle --example gallery` needs no new dependency.\n\nOne thing the gallery will surface immediately, worth expecting: `fremantle/src/lib.rs` is 7 lines of bare `pub mod` with **no re-exports**, so the example will be writing `fremantle::theme::ActiveTheme`, `fremantle::text_input::TextInput` etc. (pm-ui papers over this by re-exporting them itself in pm-ui/src/lib.rs:12,21 and pm-ui/src/theme.rs:6). Being the first real external consumer, the gallery is the natural forcing function for deciding fremantle's public prelude.\n\nAlso note the example must call `fremantle::theme::set_theme` before opening a window — `ActiveTheme::theme()` panics if the global isn't installed (theme.rs:98-105) — and fremantle ships no palette of its own, so the gallery has to define one. That makes it a second consumer of the theme, which is useful pressure on PM-18.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788593658,
          "author": "reaganiwadha",
          "kind": "status_changed",
          "old": "open",
          "new": "blocked"
        },
        {
          "at": 1788622377,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "status_changed",
          "old": "blocked",
          "new": "open"
        },
        {
          "at": 1788622388,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "commented",
          "comment_id": 1
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 79,
      "title": "fremantle + docs, fremantle needs more documentation in that it will be used by people to build ui easily. the best testcase is a rustdoc parser and a rustdoc reflector with custom embeds. It's like viewing a documentation, but built out of fremantle",
      "body": "**This ticket bundles two efforts of very different sizes.** Promoting the earlier scoping comment into the body — the recommendation there was to split, and that still stands.\n\n## Effort 1: `///` doc comments on fremantle's primitives\nNot really a ticket. Writing doc comments as each primitive lands is a habit, not a project — it should just happen in PM-73, PM-74, PM-82/83, and it's the natural cleanup pass alongside **PM-87** (which is already going through the same files removing pm-isms).\n\nCurrent state, for reference: `crates/fremantle/src/lib.rs` is 7 lines — four bare `pub mod` declarations with **no re-exports and no crate-level guidance**. `theme.rs` and `text_input.rs` do carry real module docs (text_input.rs:1-17 explains the `shape_text`/`WrappedLine` model and the IME pipeline properly). `scroll.rs` is the only module with tests (:97-134). So the docs aren't absent, they're uneven — and the missing piece is a crate-level \"here's how you use this\" rather than per-item prose.\n\n## Effort 2: a rustdoc parser + reflector with custom embeds\nThis is a **separate tool-building project**, not a documentation task — a doc-site generator that happens to be built out of fremantle so it doubles as a dogfooding testcase. It needs its own design pass before anyone starts: what it parses (rustdoc JSON output, or source?), what \"custom embeds\" means (live component previews inline in the docs?), and whether it's a fremantle example, a separate crate, or a build step.\n\nIt's also **thin on material right now**. fremantle has four modules — `scroll` (pure math), `decorations` (window chrome), `theme`, `text_input`. PM-73 (icons) and PM-74 (list primitive) are still to come. A doc-site over four modules isn't worth building yet.\n\nNote the strong overlap with **PM-9** (markdown engine): rendering rustdoc means rendering markdown, and PM-9 already lists a doc viewer as one of its consumers. If PM-9 lands in fremantle as recommended, this project gets substantially cheaper — which is another argument for waiting.\n\n## Recommendation\nRe-scope this ticket to **effort 2 only** (the doc-site tool, as a design ticket), and treat effort 1 as an ongoing habit with no ticket. Revisit once PM-73/PM-74 have landed and there's enough surface area to document — and after PM-9, if that happens.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-78** (component gallery) is the other \"show off fremantle\" ticket and is now unblocked. It's the cheaper, more immediately useful half of the same instinct — build that first and see whether a doc-site is still wanted afterwards.\n- **PM-9** — shared markdown rendering.\n- **PM-87** — the docs pass rides along with the pm-ism cleanup.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788593278,
      "updated": 1788622749,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "reaganiwadha",
          "created": 1788593658,
          "body": "Holding off on this one — it bundles two differently-sized efforts. Writing good `///` doc comments as each primitive lands is fine to just do by default, no ticket needed. A custom rustdoc parser + reflector with embeds (a doc-site generator) is a real tool-building project of its own and needs a separate design pass before anyone starts it. Also thin on material right now — fremantle only has `scroll`/`decorations` (see PM-71/PM-72 for what's coming). Recommend re-scoping/splitting this ticket, and revisiting once there's enough surface area to make a doc-site worth building.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788593658,
          "author": "reaganiwadha",
          "kind": "commented",
          "comment_id": 1
        },
        {
          "at": 1788622749,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "",
          "new": "**This ticket bundles two efforts of very different sizes.** Promoting the earlier scoping comment into the body — the recommendation there was to split, and that still stands.\n\n## Effort 1: `///` doc comments on fremantle's primitives\nNot really a ticket. Writing doc comments as each primitive lands is a habit, not a project — it should just happen in PM-73, PM-74, PM-82/83, and it's the natural cleanup pass alongside **PM-87** (which is already going through the same files removing pm-isms).\n\nCurrent state, for reference: `crates/fremantle/src/lib.rs` is 7 lines — four bare `pub mod` declarations with **no re-exports and no crate-level guidance**. `theme.rs` and `text_input.rs` do carry real module docs (text_input.rs:1-17 explains the `shape_text`/`WrappedLine` model and the IME pipeline properly). `scroll.rs` is the only module with tests (:97-134). So the docs aren't absent, they're uneven — and the missing piece is a crate-level \"here's how you use this\" rather than per-item prose.\n\n## Effort 2: a rustdoc parser + reflector with custom embeds\nThis is a **separate tool-building project**, not a documentation task — a doc-site generator that happens to be built out of fremantle so it doubles as a dogfooding testcase. It needs its own design pass before anyone starts: what it parses (rustdoc JSON output, or source?), what \"custom embeds\" means (live component previews inline in the docs?), and whether it's a fremantle example, a separate crate, or a build step.\n\nIt's also **thin on material right now**. fremantle has four modules — `scroll` (pure math), `decorations` (window chrome), `theme`, `text_input`. PM-73 (icons) and PM-74 (list primitive) are still to come. A doc-site over four modules isn't worth building yet.\n\nNote the strong overlap with **PM-9** (markdown engine): rendering rustdoc means rendering markdown, and PM-9 already lists a doc viewer as one of its consumers. If PM-9 lands in fremantle as recommended, this project gets substantially cheaper — which is another argument for waiting.\n\n## Recommendation\nRe-scope this ticket to **effort 2 only** (the doc-site tool, as a design ticket), and treat effort 1 as an ongoing habit with no ticket. Revisit once PM-73/PM-74 have landed and there's enough surface area to document — and after PM-9, if that happens.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-78** (component gallery) is the other \"show off fremantle\" ticket and is now unblocked. It's the cheaper, more immediately useful half of the same instinct — build that first and see whether a doc-site is still wanted afterwards.\n- **PM-9** — shared markdown rendering.\n- **PM-87** — the docs pass rides along with the pm-ism cleanup."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
