    {
      "id": 81,
      "title": "Focus primitives in fremantle should be first class",
      "body": "Tracking ticket. Subtickets, in order: **PM-82** (focus-ring styling helper) → **PM-83** (generic Focusable wrapper) → **PM-84** (tab / roving focus order, closes PM-28). Each gets its own plan before implementation.\n\n## Why, in one paragraph\nFocus exists in this codebase but is not a *concept*. Two unrelated shapes are in use — the widget-owns-a-handle-and-exposes-`.focus()` shape (`TextInput` alone: fremantle/src/text_input.rs:95, :180, :628) and the app-owns-a-bare-handle-and-checks-it-at-paint-time shape (`Pm::root_focus`/`diff_focus`, app.rs:133/145) — plus one inlined focus ring (text_input.rs:745/760), no `Tab` binding anywhere in the repo, and no focus order of any kind. PM-82 and PM-84 are each concretely useful on their own; PM-83 is the one to be sceptical of.\n\n## Sequencing note\nPM-83's own body flags that it may be the wrong ticket: `TextInput` is its only real consumer, and if PM-84 turns out to need just an ordered `Vec<FocusHandle>` rather than a widget wrapper, PM-83 is better closed as wontfix than half-built. Don't treat the 1→2→3 order as a commitment to build all three — re-decide after PM-82 lands.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-28** — the user-facing symptom PM-84 closes.\n- **PM-16** — \"Tab navigation\", possibly the same thing; flagged there for clarification.\n- **PM-6** — \"Cursor focus fix\", high priority but unscoped; some readings of it would close for free with this work. Flagged there.\n- **PM-64** — single-line Up/Down as Home/End. Touches the same key contexts but is independent and unblocked.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788603788,
      "updated": 1788622802,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622802,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "Tracking ticket, split into subtickets to do in order: PM-82 (focus-ring styling helper) -> PM-83 (generic Focusable widget wrapper) -> PM-84 (tab / roving focus order, closes PM-28). Each gets its own plan before implementation.",
          "new": "Tracking ticket. Subtickets, in order: **PM-82** (focus-ring styling helper) → **PM-83** (generic Focusable wrapper) → **PM-84** (tab / roving focus order, closes PM-28). Each gets its own plan before implementation.\n\n## Why, in one paragraph\nFocus exists in this codebase but is not a *concept*. Two unrelated shapes are in use — the widget-owns-a-handle-and-exposes-`.focus()` shape (`TextInput` alone: fremantle/src/text_input.rs:95, :180, :628) and the app-owns-a-bare-handle-and-checks-it-at-paint-time shape (`Pm::root_focus`/`diff_focus`, app.rs:133/145) — plus one inlined focus ring (text_input.rs:745/760), no `Tab` binding anywhere in the repo, and no focus order of any kind. PM-82 and PM-84 are each concretely useful on their own; PM-83 is the one to be sceptical of.\n\n## Sequencing note\nPM-83's own body flags that it may be the wrong ticket: `TextInput` is its only real consumer, and if PM-84 turns out to need just an ordered `Vec<FocusHandle>` rather than a widget wrapper, PM-83 is better closed as wontfix than half-built. Don't treat the 1→2→3 order as a commitment to build all three — re-decide after PM-82 lands.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-28** — the user-facing symptom PM-84 closes.\n- **PM-16** — \"Tab navigation\", possibly the same thing; flagged there for clarification.\n- **PM-6** — \"Cursor focus fix\", high priority but unscoped; some readings of it would close for free with this work. Flagged there.\n- **PM-64** — single-line Up/Down as Home/End. Touches the same key contexts but is independent and unblocked."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 82,
      "title": "fremantle: focus-ring styling helper (PM-81 1/3)",
      "body": "Part of PM-81 (1/3). The smallest, most concrete slice — do this first.\n\n## The exact code\nNow that PM-72 has landed, this lives in `crates/fremantle/src/text_input.rs`:\n- `:745` — `let focused = self.focus_handle.is_focused(window);`\n- `:760` — `.border_color(if focused { colors.select } else { colors.border })`\n\nThat two-line pattern is the entire focus-ring implementation in the codebase, inlined into the one widget that has focus styling at all.\n\n## In scope\nExtract it into a reusable fremantle helper so the next primitive doesn't re-derive it — either a small style fn taking `bool`, or a `Styled` extension (`.focus_ring(focused, cx)`). Then use it from `TextInput`.\n\nKeep it genuinely small. This ticket is deliberately the mechanical one; the abstraction question (\"what should own focus?\") belongs to **PM-83**, and building the wrapper here would defeat the point of the split.\n\n## Worth noting while you're in there\n`TextInput` reads its focus state in **two** places, not one — the render path above, and `TextElement::paint`, where the caret is gated on focus: `let focus = self.input.read(cx).focus_handle.clone();` (:1014) then `if focus.is_focused(window) {` (:1032). Only the render-path one is a *styling* concern and in scope here, but the second is useful context for PM-83, which has to account for both.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-83** (Focusable wrapper) consumes this.\n- **PM-18** — `colors.select` and `colors.border` come from `ThemeColors` (fremantle/src/theme.rs:22-33), so the ring is already themeable. Good; keep it that way rather than hardcoding.\n\n## Verify\n`cargo check`; focus and blur each of the five text inputs in pm-ui (new-ticket title, new-ticket body, comment box, ticket search, the author box in the status-bar popover — all constructed at app.rs:210-232) and confirm the ring behaves exactly as before. This is a pure refactor: no visual change is the success condition.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "fremantle"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788604310,
      "updated": 1788622681,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622681,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "TextInput (crates/fremantle/src/text_input.rs) inlines its own focused-border-color check: `let focused = self.focus_handle.is_focused(window);` then `.border_color(if focused { colors.select } else { colors.border })`. Extract this into a small reusable fremantle helper (e.g. a style fn taking `bool` or a `Styled` extension) so any future fremantle widget gets consistent focus-ring styling without re-deriving the pattern. Smallest, concrete slice of PM-81 -- do this first.",
          "new": "Part of PM-81 (1/3). The smallest, most concrete slice — do this first.\n\n## The exact code\nNow that PM-72 has landed, this lives in `crates/fremantle/src/text_input.rs`:\n- `:745` — `let focused = self.focus_handle.is_focused(window);`\n- `:760` — `.border_color(if focused { colors.select } else { colors.border })`\n\nThat two-line pattern is the entire focus-ring implementation in the codebase, inlined into the one widget that has focus styling at all.\n\n## In scope\nExtract it into a reusable fremantle helper so the next primitive doesn't re-derive it — either a small style fn taking `bool`, or a `Styled` extension (`.focus_ring(focused, cx)`). Then use it from `TextInput`.\n\nKeep it genuinely small. This ticket is deliberately the mechanical one; the abstraction question (\"what should own focus?\") belongs to **PM-83**, and building the wrapper here would defeat the point of the split.\n\n## Worth noting while you're in there\n`TextInput` reads its focus state in **two** places, not one — the render path above, and `TextElement::paint`, where the caret is gated on focus: `let focus = self.input.read(cx).focus_handle.clone();` (:1014) then `if focus.is_focused(window) {` (:1032). Only the render-path one is a *styling* concern and in scope here, but the second is useful context for PM-83, which has to account for both.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-83** (Focusable wrapper) consumes this.\n- **PM-18** — `colors.select` and `colors.border` come from `ThemeColors` (fremantle/src/theme.rs:22-33), so the ring is already themeable. Good; keep it that way rather than hardcoding.\n\n## Verify\n`cargo check`; focus and blur each of the five text inputs in pm-ui (new-ticket title, new-ticket body, comment box, ticket search, the author box in the status-bar popover — all constructed at app.rs:210-232) and confirm the ring behaves exactly as before. This is a pure refactor: no visual change is the success condition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 83,
      "title": "fremantle: generic Focusable widget wrapper (PM-83 2/3)",
      "body": "Part of PM-81 (2/3), after PM-82. **Worth a plan before touching code** — this risks over-engineering an abstraction with one real consumer.\n\n## The inventory a wrapper has to absorb\nFocus is currently handled in two structurally different ways, and any generic wrapper has to cover both — this is the thing that makes the design non-obvious:\n\n**Shape A — the widget owns a handle and exposes `.focus()`.** Only `TextInput` does this:\n- `focus_handle: FocusHandle` field (fremantle/src/text_input.rs:95), created in `build` (:132)\n- `pub fn focus(&self, window, cx)` (:180-182)\n- `impl Focusable` (:628-632)\n- `.key_context(ctx).track_focus(&self.focus_handle)` in render (:752-753)\n- read twice: for the ring (:745, see PM-82) and to gate caret painting in `TextElement::paint` (:1014, :1032)\n\nCallers drive it imperatively — `ti.focus(window, cx)` from tickets_view.rs:83 (Ctrl+F), :206 (search toggle), :356 (list interaction).\n\n**Shape B — the app owns a bare handle and checks it at paint time.** `Pm` holds two:\n- `diff_focus` (app.rs:133) — `.track_focus` at :1403, `.on_key_down` at :1404, read in diff_view.rs:448 to gate the caret, focused on mouse-down at diff_view.rs:886\n- `root_focus` (app.rs:145) — `.track_focus` at :920, focused at window open (src/main.rs:226). Its doc (:143) explains why it exists: \"so menu actions / keybindings have a dispatch path even before the diff pane is focused\"\n\nA wrapper that only handles Shape A is a `TextInput` helper wearing a trait's clothes. Handling Shape B too is what would make it worth building.\n\n## Key contexts — the part that actually matters for PM-84\nOnly `TextInput` declares a `key_context` (`\"TextInput TextInputMultiLine\"` / `\"TextInput TextInputSingleLine\"`, :746-750). The root and diff panes declare **none**, so every app-level action is bound with context `None` (src/main.rs:82-95). That's fine today and becomes a problem the moment PM-84 wants a `Tab` binding — see that ticket.\n\n## The honest scope question\nTextInput is the only current consumer, and fremantle has no other focusable primitive. Two defensible answers:\n- **Build the wrapper now**, so PM-74's list primitive and PM-78's gallery arrive into a focus model rather than inventing one each.\n- **Skip the wrapper**, do PM-82's helper plus PM-84's tab order directly, and revisit when there's a second real consumer.\n\nRecommend deciding this explicitly at plan time rather than defaulting to \"build it because there's a ticket\". If PM-84 turns out to need only a focus *order* (an ordered list of `FocusHandle`s) and not a widget wrapper, this ticket may be better closed as wontfix than half-built.\n\n## Verify\n`cargo check`; all five text inputs (app.rs:210-232) plus the diff pane keep their existing focus behaviour — ring, caret gating, click-to-focus, and Ctrl+F reaching the search box. Pure refactor: no behaviour change is the success condition.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "fremantle"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788604310,
      "updated": 1788622696,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622696,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "Part of PM-81 (2/3), after PM-82. Build a broader fremantle abstraction (e.g. a `Focusable` wrapper component) that any primitive can compose for focus tracking + the PM-82 ring styling + tab semantics, even though TextInput is currently the only real consumer. More architecture than immediate payoff -- worth a plan before touching code, since it risks over-engineering a one-consumer abstraction.",
          "new": "Part of PM-81 (2/3), after PM-82. **Worth a plan before touching code** — this risks over-engineering an abstraction with one real consumer.\n\n## The inventory a wrapper has to absorb\nFocus is currently handled in two structurally different ways, and any generic wrapper has to cover both — this is the thing that makes the design non-obvious:\n\n**Shape A — the widget owns a handle and exposes `.focus()`.** Only `TextInput` does this:\n- `focus_handle: FocusHandle` field (fremantle/src/text_input.rs:95), created in `build` (:132)\n- `pub fn focus(&self, window, cx)` (:180-182)\n- `impl Focusable` (:628-632)\n- `.key_context(ctx).track_focus(&self.focus_handle)` in render (:752-753)\n- read twice: for the ring (:745, see PM-82) and to gate caret painting in `TextElement::paint` (:1014, :1032)\n\nCallers drive it imperatively — `ti.focus(window, cx)` from tickets_view.rs:83 (Ctrl+F), :206 (search toggle), :356 (list interaction).\n\n**Shape B — the app owns a bare handle and checks it at paint time.** `Pm` holds two:\n- `diff_focus` (app.rs:133) — `.track_focus` at :1403, `.on_key_down` at :1404, read in diff_view.rs:448 to gate the caret, focused on mouse-down at diff_view.rs:886\n- `root_focus` (app.rs:145) — `.track_focus` at :920, focused at window open (src/main.rs:226). Its doc (:143) explains why it exists: \"so menu actions / keybindings have a dispatch path even before the diff pane is focused\"\n\nA wrapper that only handles Shape A is a `TextInput` helper wearing a trait's clothes. Handling Shape B too is what would make it worth building.\n\n## Key contexts — the part that actually matters for PM-84\nOnly `TextInput` declares a `key_context` (`\"TextInput TextInputMultiLine\"` / `\"TextInput TextInputSingleLine\"`, :746-750). The root and diff panes declare **none**, so every app-level action is bound with context `None` (src/main.rs:82-95). That's fine today and becomes a problem the moment PM-84 wants a `Tab` binding — see that ticket.\n\n## The honest scope question\nTextInput is the only current consumer, and fremantle has no other focusable primitive. Two defensible answers:\n- **Build the wrapper now**, so PM-74's list primitive and PM-78's gallery arrive into a focus model rather than inventing one each.\n- **Skip the wrapper**, do PM-82's helper plus PM-84's tab order directly, and revisit when there's a second real consumer.\n\nRecommend deciding this explicitly at plan time rather than defaulting to \"build it because there's a ticket\". If PM-84 turns out to need only a focus *order* (an ordered list of `FocusHandle`s) and not a widget wrapper, this ticket may be better closed as wontfix than half-built.\n\n## Verify\n`cargo check`; all five text inputs (app.rs:210-232) plus the diff pane keep their existing focus behaviour — ring, caret gating, click-to-focus, and Ctrl+F reaching the search box. Pure refactor: no behaviour change is the success condition."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 84,
      "title": "Tickets: tab / roving focus order between inputs (PM-81 3/3, closes PM-28)",
      "body": "Part of PM-81 (3/3), after PM-83. Closes **PM-28** (\"Can't tab between inputs like Title to Description\").\n\n## Two hard constraints, neither previously recorded\n\n**1. There is no `Tab` binding anywhere in the repo.** Grep confirms: no `Tab`/`ShiftTab` action, no focus-order registry, no `tab_index`/`tab_stop` usage at all. The only tab bindings are `ctrl-tab`/`ctrl-shift-tab` → `NextView`/`PrevView` for top-level view cycling (src/main.rs:89-90, `cycle_view` at app.rs:504-513). So this is built from nothing, not extended.\n\n**2. A naive `Tab` binding would steal Tab from the diff view.** Only `TextInput` declares a `key_context` (fremantle/src/text_input.rs:746-750); the root (`.track_focus` app.rs:920) and the diff pane (:1403) declare none, and every app-level binding is registered with context `None` (src/main.rs:82-95). A context-less `Tab` would fire everywhere, including inside the diff body, which has its own `on_key_down` (app.rs:762 `on_diff_key`).\n\n**So the first task is a key context, not a focus order.** Either scope the binding to `TextInput` (the context already exists, via `bind_text_input_keys`, src/main.rs:145-178) or introduce a form/pane context. Note Tab has a second meaning inside a multiline input — literal indentation — so `TextInputMultiLine` may want to keep it while `TextInputSingleLine` moves focus. Decide deliberately.\n\n## The inputs needing an order\nFive `Entity<TextInput>` fields, all built in `Pm::new` (app.rs:210-232):\n- `new_ticket_title` (:210, single) — already emits `Submit` → `submit_new_ticket` (:211-215)\n- `new_ticket_body` (:216-217, multi)\n- `comment_box` (:218, multi)\n- `ticket_search` (:219, single) — subscribed for `Changed` (:222-226)\n- `author_box` (:231-241, single) — lives in the status-bar popover (status_bar.rs:287), not the tickets pane\n\nThey are **not all visible at once**: the new-ticket form (tickets_view.rs:493-546) and the ticket detail card (:603-770) are mutually exclusive branches, and `ticket_search` only mounts when the search toggle is open (:441-449). So a static global order is wrong — the order has to be computed from what's currently mounted, which is the main design problem in this ticket.\n\n`author_box` is in a popover and probably shouldn't join the main order at all.\n\n## Depends on\n**PM-83** (Focusable wrapper) — for knowing what is focusable and in what order. Note PM-83's own body flags that it may be closed as wontfix if the wrapper isn't warranted; if that happens, this ticket does the job directly with an ordered `Vec<FocusHandle>` and PM-82's ring helper.\n\n## Closes\n**PM-28** — same bug, user-facing phrasing. Close it when this lands. (**PM-16** \"Tab navigation\" also looks like the same thing; see the note on that ticket.)\n\n## Open question\nShould Tab wrap around at the end of the form, or fall through to the next pane? Wrapping is simpler and is what a modal-ish form wants; falling through needs a whole-app focus order, which is a much larger ticket.\n\n## Verify\nIn the new-ticket form, Tab moves Title → Description and Shift+Tab back; with the search box open, Tab reaches it; the ring (PM-82) follows focus; Enter in the title still submits (app.rs:211-215); and Tab inside the diff view still does whatever it did before, unchanged.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "fremantle",
        "tickets"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "reaganiwadha",
      "created": 1788604310,
      "updated": 1788622717,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622717,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "body_changed",
          "old": "Part of PM-81 (3/3), after PM-83. Keyboard tab-order between multiple focusable elements in a form (e.g. Tickets search box, new-ticket title/body fields). Closes PM-28 (\"Can't tab between inputs like Title to Description\"). Depends on the Focusable wrapper from PM-83 to know what's focusable and in what order.",
          "new": "Part of PM-81 (3/3), after PM-83. Closes **PM-28** (\"Can't tab between inputs like Title to Description\").\n\n## Two hard constraints, neither previously recorded\n\n**1. There is no `Tab` binding anywhere in the repo.** Grep confirms: no `Tab`/`ShiftTab` action, no focus-order registry, no `tab_index`/`tab_stop` usage at all. The only tab bindings are `ctrl-tab`/`ctrl-shift-tab` → `NextView`/`PrevView` for top-level view cycling (src/main.rs:89-90, `cycle_view` at app.rs:504-513). So this is built from nothing, not extended.\n\n**2. A naive `Tab` binding would steal Tab from the diff view.** Only `TextInput` declares a `key_context` (fremantle/src/text_input.rs:746-750); the root (`.track_focus` app.rs:920) and the diff pane (:1403) declare none, and every app-level binding is registered with context `None` (src/main.rs:82-95). A context-less `Tab` would fire everywhere, including inside the diff body, which has its own `on_key_down` (app.rs:762 `on_diff_key`).\n\n**So the first task is a key context, not a focus order.** Either scope the binding to `TextInput` (the context already exists, via `bind_text_input_keys`, src/main.rs:145-178) or introduce a form/pane context. Note Tab has a second meaning inside a multiline input — literal indentation — so `TextInputMultiLine` may want to keep it while `TextInputSingleLine` moves focus. Decide deliberately.\n\n## The inputs needing an order\nFive `Entity<TextInput>` fields, all built in `Pm::new` (app.rs:210-232):\n- `new_ticket_title` (:210, single) — already emits `Submit` → `submit_new_ticket` (:211-215)\n- `new_ticket_body` (:216-217, multi)\n- `comment_box` (:218, multi)\n- `ticket_search` (:219, single) — subscribed for `Changed` (:222-226)\n- `author_box` (:231-241, single) — lives in the status-bar popover (status_bar.rs:287), not the tickets pane\n\nThey are **not all visible at once**: the new-ticket form (tickets_view.rs:493-546) and the ticket detail card (:603-770) are mutually exclusive branches, and `ticket_search` only mounts when the search toggle is open (:441-449). So a static global order is wrong — the order has to be computed from what's currently mounted, which is the main design problem in this ticket.\n\n`author_box` is in a popover and probably shouldn't join the main order at all.\n\n## Depends on\n**PM-83** (Focusable wrapper) — for knowing what is focusable and in what order. Note PM-83's own body flags that it may be closed as wontfix if the wrapper isn't warranted; if that happens, this ticket does the job directly with an ordered `Vec<FocusHandle>` and PM-82's ring helper.\n\n## Closes\n**PM-28** — same bug, user-facing phrasing. Close it when this lands. (**PM-16** \"Tab navigation\" also looks like the same thing; see the note on that ticket.)\n\n## Open question\nShould Tab wrap around at the end of the form, or fall through to the next pane? Wrapping is simpler and is what a modal-ish form wants; falling through needs a whole-app focus order, which is a much larger ticket.\n\n## Verify\nIn the new-ticket form, Tab moves Title → Description and Shift+Tab back; with the search box open, Tab reaches it; the ring (PM-82) follows focus; Enter in the title still submits (app.rs:211-215); and Tab inside the diff view still does whatever it did before, unchanged."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": 85,
      "title": "pm-core/AppState: expose ticket field mutators (title/body/priority/labels/assignee)",
      "body": "The shared blocker behind PM-4, PM-8, PM-38 and PM-57. Each of those is independently stuck on the same small gap, and none of them said so — hence this ticket.\n\n## The gap\n`PmData` already has every mutator, each one touching `updated` and pushing the right `HistoryEvent`, each returning `bool` for \"did anything change\":\n- `set_title` — crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:398\n- `set_body` — :423\n- `set_priority` — :448\n- `set_assignee` — :472\n- `set_labels` — :496\n\nBut `AppState` wraps only three of them: `create_ticket` (state.rs:229), `add_comment` (:243), `set_ticket_status` (:252). The other five have **no `AppState` wrapper and no UI call site**, which is why status is the only editable field in the entire Tickets pane (`status_button`, tickets_view.rs:549-601) — title, body, priority, labels and assignee all render read-only (tickets_view.rs:619-654).\n\nNote the asymmetry: the MCP `edit_ticket` tool already reaches all five (pm-mcp/src/ops.rs:164-224). An agent can edit a ticket's title; the person sitting in front of the GUI cannot.\n\n## In scope\nFive wrappers on `AppState`, each mirroring `set_ticket_status` (state.rs:252-262) exactly: resolve the author via `author_or_default` (:220), call the `PmData` mutator with `pm::now_unix()`, and `save_pm()` only when it returned `true`. That's the whole change — no new types, no schema change.\n\n## Out of scope\nThe UI affordances themselves. Each consuming ticket builds its own: PM-57 (click-to-edit title), PM-4 (priority picker), PM-38 (label editor), PM-8 (body editing alongside markdown).\n\n## Worth knowing for whoever picks this up\n`save_pm` (state.rs:211) rewrites the entire pm.json5 on every call, and there are three concurrent writers (GUI, MCP server, the user's editor) reconciled only by the retry loop in `pm::load_in` (pm.rs:270) and the Sentinel debounce (watch.rs:69). Five new save paths don't change that model, but a click-to-edit field that saves per keystroke would hammer it — consumers should save on commit/blur, not on `Changed`.\n\n## Verify\n`cargo check`; then drive one of them from a consuming ticket and confirm the edit persists to .pm/pm.json5 **and** appears in the ticket's History section (tickets_view.rs:687-707) with the right author — the history entry is the real proof the wrapper went through `PmData` rather than mutating the struct directly.",
      "status": "done",
      "priority": "high",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "claude",
      "created": 1788622400,
      "updated": 1788625537,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788625534,
          "body": "Shipped. Added five wrappers on `AppState` (`crates/pm-core/src/state.rs`, after `set_ticket_status`): `set_ticket_title`, `set_ticket_body`, `set_ticket_priority`, `set_ticket_assignee`, `set_ticket_labels`. Each mirrors `set_ticket_status` exactly — `author_or_default`, the `PmData` mutator with `pm::now_unix()`, `save_pm()` only on `true`. No new types, no schema change, no UI call sites (those belong to PM-4/8/38/57).\n\nNew unit test `ticket_field_wrappers_persist_and_record_history` in state.rs: drives all five, asserts field values, a matching `HistoryEvent` per field with the resolved author, persistence to `.pm/pm.json5`, and that a no-op edit pushes no history entry. `cargo test -p pm-core` green (27 passed); clippy clean (the 2 remaining warnings are pre-existing in highlight.rs).",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": [
