    {
      "id": 86,
      "title": "pm.json5: `version` is written but never read — an older binary silently drops newer fields",
      "body": "A data-loss hazard that becomes real as soon as the schema grows. Filed now because four open tickets are about to grow it.\n\n## What's actually there\n`PmData.version: u32` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:212, `#[serde(default = \"one\")]`) and `Config.version` (config.rs:29) are both written on every save and **neither is ever read**. There is no migration function, no `match data.version`, no `migrate`/`upgrade` symbol anywhere in the repo. The hook exists; the machinery behind it does not.\n\nForward-compatibility today rests entirely on serde's defaults:\n- Every field on `Ticket` and `Comment` after `id` is `#[serde(default)]` (pm.rs:167-196), so *older* files load into a *newer* binary fine. That direction is genuinely safe.\n- But nothing uses `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]`, so unknown keys are **silently discarded on read**, and `to_pretty` (pm.rs:295) always rewrites the whole document from the in-memory struct.\n\nSo the reverse direction destroys data: an older `pm` opens a file written by a newer one, drops every field it doesn't know, and the next save — any save, including one triggered by merely changing a ticket's status — writes the truncated document back. No error, no warning, no way to tell it happened afterwards.\n\n## Why now\nEvery schema-extension ticket in the current backlog adds exactly the kind of field this eats: PM-69 (`Comment.parent`), PM-50 (ticket links), PM-38 (tags, if it doesn't reuse `labels`), PM-33 (attachments). And the exposure is wider than one machine — pm.json5 is committed to the repo, so a teammate on an older release, or a stale `pm --mcp` server left running, is enough. Three writers already share the file: the GUI (`AppState::save_pm`, state.rs:211), the MCP server (ops.rs:59, serialized only by a process-local mutex, ops.rs:21), and the user's editor.\n\n## Suggested first slice (cheap, stops the bleeding)\nRead `version` in `load_in` (pm.rs:267). If it's greater than the version this binary knows, surface it and refuse to save — the GUI already has a channel for this in `AppState::pm_error` (state.rs:102), rendered as a banner at tickets_view.rs:466-475, and pm-mcp can return it as a tool error via `reply()` (lib.rs:35-42). Read-only is a fine, honest degradation; silent truncation is not.\n\n## Later, if wanted\nAn actual migration ladder (`version` N→N+1 functions run on load), and/or preserving unknown keys round-trip via a `#[serde(flatten)] extra: Map<String, Value>` catch-all on `Ticket`/`Comment` so an old binary carries new fields through untouched instead of dropping them. The catch-all is the stronger fix and is worth considering *before* the first schema addition lands, since it only helps for versions written after it exists.\n\n## Open question\nShould a version bump be required for additive-only changes at all? If the catch-all lands, additive changes stop being breaking, and `version` would only need to move for genuinely incompatible reshapes.\n\n## Verify\nUnit test in pm.rs (alongside the existing round-trip tests at :549-631): write a document containing an unknown key plus a bumped `version`, load it, save it, and assert the unknown key survives / the save is refused — whichever behaviour is chosen.",
      "status": "done",
      "priority": "high",
      "labels": [],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "claude",
      "created": 1788622421,
      "updated": 1788626935,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788626918,
          "body": "Shipped the \"cheap first slice\" — refuse to save a store newer than we understand.\n\n- `pm-core/src/pm.rs`: new `pub const SCHEMA_VERSION: u32 = 1`. `PmData::save_in` bails if `self.version > SCHEMA_VERSION` — the single chokepoint every writer goes through (GUI `save_pm`, MCP `save`, `PmData::save`). Reads are untouched: a v2 file still parses and its tickets load, so the data stays intact in memory; it just never gets written back and truncated.\n- `pm-core/src/state.rs`: `schema_too_new()` helper; `AppState::new` and `reload_pm` set `pm_error` (the existing banner channel, tickets_view.rs:466) when the loaded store is too new, so the GUI shows read-only immediately rather than only after a failed save.\n- MCP: no explicit change needed — a mutating op's `save` bails and surfaces via `reply()` as a tool error.\n- Tests in pm.rs: `newer_schema_loads_but_wont_save` (v2 file loads, save refused, unknown field on disk survives) and `current_schema_saves`.\n\nDeferred (the ticket's \"later\" section): the migration ladder and the `#[serde(flatten)] extra` round-trip catch-all. The catch-all is the stronger fix and only helps for versions written after it lands — worth doing before the first real schema addition (PM-69/50/33). Leaving this ticket open or splitting it is your call; the bleeding is stopped.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788622421,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "priority_changed",
          "old": "normal",
          "new": "high"
        },
        {
          "at": 1788626918,
          "author": "claude",
