    {
      "id": 89,
      "title": "Anchors as a first-class feature — the shared substrate under tickets and line comments",
      "body": "**Deliberately loose.** Capturing the shape of the idea, not a spec. Anchors are a core pm feature that currently has no owner ticket — PM-2 covers one entry point, but the concept itself spans tickets, comments, and the MCP surface, and the design questions below want answering *once* rather than three times.\n\n## The pitch\npm is \"Jira, direct-to-code\". An anchor is what makes that literal: a ticket or a comment that points at `file:start-end@rev` rather than describing a location in prose. Everything else in pm is a tracker; anchors are the part that isn't.\n\n## What exists\nThe model is already there and has been for a while: `Anchor { file, start_line, end_line, rev }` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:108-116), where `rev` is a short commit id or the literal `\"working\"`. Two carriers:\n- `Ticket.anchors: Vec<Anchor>` (:189) — a ticket can point at several places\n- `Comment.anchor: Option<Anchor>` (:132) — a comment points at one\n\nWhat's missing is everything around it:\n- **Nothing creates them.** The model's own doc (:104) says \"Not created by the UI yet — the diff-gutter flow comes later\". That flow is PM-2.\n- **The MCP surface can't touch them.** `create_ticket` and `edit_ticket` have no `anchors` arg, `add_comment` has no `anchor` arg (crates/pm-mcp/src/lib.rs:61-97). So an agent — the actor that most reliably knows the exact file and line — has no way to record it. Every ticket in this backlog puts `pm.rs:398` in prose because that was the only option.\n- **They don't navigate properly.** Anchors render read-only as `📎 file:start–end` (tickets_view.rs:662-683), but clicking one calls `open_path`, which resets scroll to zero (app.rs:435-445). Every anchor is a file link pretending to be a line link. (Tracked as slice 1 of PM-2.)\n\n## The questions worth thinking about\n- **Staleness.** An anchor captured against `\"working\"` is wrong the moment the file is edited. Do anchors re-anchor themselves (track content, fuzzy-match on reopen), or do they go visibly stale and let you fix them? Both are defensible; doing neither means anchors silently rot, which is the worst option and the current default.\n- **What is `rev` for?** Pinning to a commit makes an anchor permanently correct but increasingly irrelevant. Pinning to `\"working\"` keeps it relevant and lets it drift. Is one the default, or does it depend on whether you're filing a bug (pin it) or a task (follow it)?\n- **Are line-comments and anchored-tickets the same feature?** `Comment.anchor` and `Ticket.anchors` suggest not quite. A review comment on a diff line and a ticket that points at some code feel different in use but share a data shape entirely. Worth deciding whether they converge or stay deliberately separate.\n- **Is an anchor navigable in its own right?** e.g. \"show me everything anchored to this file\", or gutter markers in the diff view showing that a line already has tickets/comments on it. That would make anchors *discoverable* rather than only findable from the ticket side — arguably where most of the value is.\n- **Range semantics.** Do anchors survive a range being partly deleted? Split? What does a 400-line anchor even mean (PM-2's own anchor spans `diff_view.rs:698-970`)?\n\n## Entry points that would need to write them\n1. The diff gutter — **PM-2**.\n2. The MCP tools — agents finding a problem while reading code. Probably the highest-volume producer in practice, and currently the only one with no path at all.\n3. Possibly: selecting text in the diff and filing from there; a \"file a ticket about this\" from the file tree.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-2** — the gutter flow and the scroll-to-line fix. The nearest-term slice.\n- **PM-69** — nested comments; a reply to an anchored comment presumably inherits or ignores the anchor.\n- **PM-50** — links are the ticket→ticket graph; anchors are the ticket→code graph. Same instinct, different axis.\n- **PM-9 / PM-8** — if bodies render markdown, an anchor could render as a real code excerpt inline rather than a path string.\n- **PM-86** — anchors written by a newer binary are droppable by an older one.\n\n## No verification section\nBecause there's nothing specified yet. This ticket wants a design pass and a split into real subtickets, the way PM-81 and PM-55 were split.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "high",
      "labels": [
        "mcp",
        "tickets"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "claude",
      "created": 1788624529,
      "updated": 1788624529,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
        {
          "at": 1788624529,
          "author": "claude",
          "kind": "priority_changed",
          "old": "normal",
          "new": "high"
        },
