    {
      "id": 90,
      "title": "MCP: rewrite the server instructions to say *when* to use pm, not just which tools exist",
      "body": "Cheapest change on the list, probably the biggest behaviour change. Part of what PM-76's \"Needs Claude Code input\" was asking for.\n\n## What it says today\n`crates/pm-mcp/src/lib.rs:194-200`, one hardcoded paragraph:\n\n> \"Ticket tracker for a pm project (in-repo .pm/pm.json5). Tools: list_tickets, get_ticket, add_comment, create_ticket, edit_ticket, open_project, list_projects. Authorship is a free, unverified string — pass `author` to attribute work to a specific name.\"\n\nIt describes the *surface* and nothing else. An agent reading that learns pm exists and what it can call. It learns nothing about when any of it is appropriate — so pm gets used when the user explicitly says \"file a ticket\", and ignored otherwise.\n\n## What's missing\nConcretely, the things an agent should be told and currently isn't:\n- **Check before you start.** Search for an existing ticket before beginning a piece of work, and before filing a new one. (Needs the search tool to exist — see the search ticket.)\n- **Record what you learned.** A comment on the ticket is the right home for scoping notes, dead ends, and decisions. PM-44's scoping comment is the model: an implementation was attempted, reverted, and the *reason* survived. That's the behaviour to ask for explicitly.\n- **Close what you finish.** This backlog had four tickets whose work had shipped and merged while they sat `open`. An agent that just implemented PM-N should close PM-N.\n- **Say who you are.** Pass `author` — otherwise every agent edit is attributed to the resolved fallback (config author → git `user.name` → `\"unknown\"`, crates/pm-core/src/identity.rs:16-28), i.e. it looks like the human wrote it.\n- **Ticket ids are the shared vocabulary.** `PM-N` in a commit message, a body, or a comment refers to a real object; use it consistently.\n- **Don't file duplicates.** With no search and no dedupe this is currently just a hope; worth stating anyway.\n\n## Also worth fixing while in here\n`ServerInfo::default()` (lib.rs:192) is used for the server name and version — so pm reports rmcp's defaults rather than `\"pm\"` and `pm_core::buildinfo::VERSION`. An agent can't tell which pm it's talking to, which matters more once PM-88 ships a second binary.\n\nPer-tool descriptions (lib.rs:126-186) are also thin and in one case actively misleading — see the `list_tickets` bulk-read ticket.\n\n## Open questions\n- How prescriptive should this be? Instructions that read as a rigid workflow get ignored or followed too literally; instructions that read as context get used well. Lean toward describing what pm is *for* and letting the agent infer, rather than issuing a numbered procedure.\n- Should it differ per project? It's currently global and static (one `&'static str`), so it can't say anything about *this* repo's conventions. A project-level hint file is the other half of PM-76 and might be the better home for anything repo-specific.\n- Does length hurt? This string is prepended to agent context every session. Worth keeping it tight rather than exhaustive.\n\n## Verify\nHard to test mechanically. The real check is behavioural: start a fresh agent session on a pm project, give it a task that would benefit from ticketing, and see whether it looks for an existing ticket and closes what it finishes without being told to.",
      "status": "done",
      "priority": "high",
      "labels": [
        "mcp"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "claude",
      "created": 1788624552,
      "updated": 1788626932,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [
        {
          "id": 1,
          "author": "claude",
          "created": 1788626908,
          "body": "Done. `crates/pm-mcp/src/lib.rs` `get_info`:\n- `info.server_info = Implementation::new(\"pm\", pm_core::buildinfo::VERSION)` — the handshake now reports `{\"name\":\"pm\",\"version\":\"0.0.5\"}` instead of rmcp's `env!(\"CARGO_CRATE_NAME\")` default. Verified with a raw `initialize` request.\n- Instructions rewritten from the tool-list blurb to context about *when* to use pm: check for an existing ticket before starting/filing, set status `done` when you finish a ticket's work, leave scoping/dead-end notes as comments, file tickets for follow-ups you spot, and always pass `author`. Framed as context, not a numbered procedure, per the ticket's open question. Kept to ~1KB.\n\nNot done here: the \"search before you start\" line points at `list_tickets` since there's still no text search — PM-91. Per-tool descriptions and the project-specific-hint question (PM-76) left alone.",
          "anchor": null
        }
      ],
