    {
      "id": 92,
      "title": "MCP: let list_tickets return bodies in bulk (and fix its misleading description)",
      "body": "## The gap, with a worked example\n`list_tickets` returns id, title, status, priority, author, labels, assignee, updated and comment *count* — no bodies. `get_ticket` (lib.rs:132-136) returns one ticket in full. There is nothing in between.\n\nSo surveying a backlog costs N round-trips. Grooming this project on 2026-09-05 needed the bodies of ~50 open tickets; the honest outcome is that **MCP was abandoned for that step** and `.pm/pm.json5` was read straight off disk with a Python one-liner instead. A tool that agents route around for the most common bulk operation isn't doing its job.\n\n## Two fixes\n**1. An opt-in bulk read.** Either `include_bodies: bool` or a general `fields` parameter on `list_tickets`. Opt-in matters — the default response should stay small, since dumping 89 full bodies into context unprompted is its own problem. A `truncate` / excerpt length would make the middle ground usable: enough body to judge relevance, not the whole thing.\n\n**2. Fix the description.** It currently reads *\"List tickets in a pm project (id, title, status, priority, author).\"* (lib.rs:126). That's a promise about the response shape which is *nearly* right and therefore worse than vague — it omits labels, assignee, updated and comment count, which it does return, and it doesn't say bodies are excluded, which is the thing a caller needs to know. Whatever shape this lands in, the description should state exactly what comes back and what doesn't.\n\n## Response-shape decision, shared with the search ticket\nThe MCP search tool wants the same thing — hits with enough excerpt to judge relevance. Settle one shape and use it for both rather than inventing two.\n\n## Worth checking while in here\nThe other tool descriptions are similarly thin (lib.rs:126-186). `create_ticket` doesn't mention that `priority` defaults to Normal or that ids are per-project; `edit_ticket` doesn't say that omitted fields are left alone (they are — ops.rs:164-224) which is genuinely non-obvious for a tool named \"edit\"; `list_projects` doesn't mention its depth default of 3 or that, unlike every other tool, it does **not** walk up to find a project root (lib.rs:181-185).\n\n## Verify\nOne `list_tickets` call with bodies enabled returns the whole backlog in a form an agent can survey without follow-ups — and the default call is no larger than it is today.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "mcp"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "claude",
      "created": 1788624584,
      "updated": 1788624584,
