    {
      "id": 93,
      "title": "MCP: create_ticket should warn on likely duplicates instead of silently filing one",
      "body": "## The gap\n`create_ticket` (crates/pm-mcp/src/lib.rs:144-155, impl ops.rs:136-162) appends unconditionally. No similarity check, no warning, no confirmation step. Whoever calls it gets a new `PM-N` every time.\n\nThat's fine for a human typing a title they just thought of. It's poor for an agent, which files tickets in bulk during a grooming or planning pass and has no cheap way to know what's already there — see the search ticket, which is the missing capability underneath this one.\n\nDuplicates are already present in this backlog by hand: **PM-16** (\"Tab navigation\") looks like a duplicate of **PM-28** (\"Can't tab between inputs\"), and **PM-9** vs **PM-8** needed an explicit split to stop being one. Adding an agent that files at speed to a store with no dedupe makes this steadily worse.\n\n## Shape\nOn `create_ticket`, run the same match the search tool uses against existing titles and bodies. If anything scores above a threshold, return the candidates **instead of creating**, with a flag the caller can set to proceed anyway (`force: true`, or a returned token to confirm with).\n\nThe important property is that the default is safe and the override is explicit — an agent that genuinely wants a near-duplicate can say so in one extra call, and one that didn't realise gets told.\n\n## Open questions\n- **Threshold and scope.** Title-only matching is predictable but misses rephrasings; including bodies catches more and produces more false positives. Since the response is \"here are candidates, decide\", false positives are cheap and misses are not — lean permissive.\n- **Should this apply to the GUI too?** A human typing a title into the new-ticket form (tickets_view.rs:493-546) would benefit from the same \"3 similar tickets\" hint, shown live rather than as a rejection. Arguably the better UX, and it's the same matcher. Possibly a separate ticket.\n- Should closed tickets count? Filing a duplicate of something already `wontfix` is worth knowing about, and it's arguably the more useful catch.\n\n## Depends on\nThe MCP search/query tool — this is that matcher run automatically at file time. Don't build a second one.\n\n## Verify\nAttempt to file a ticket titled close to an existing one (e.g. \"Tab navigation between inputs\") and confirm it returns PM-16 and PM-28 as candidates rather than creating PM-92. Confirm the override still files it.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "mcp"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "claude",
      "created": 1788624599,
      "updated": 1788624599,
      "anchors": [],
