    {
      "id": 94,
      "title": "MCP: batch edits in one call (a grooming pass is ~30 sequential round-trips today)",
      "body": "## The gap\nEvery mutating tool takes exactly one ticket: `edit_ticket`, `add_comment`, `create_ticket`. A grooming pass over this backlog on 2026-09-05 took roughly **30 sequential MCP calls** — 4 status changes, ~20 body rewrites, ~10 comments — each a full round-trip, and each one loading, mutating and rewriting the entire `pm.json5`.\n\n## Why it's more than an ergonomics complaint\n`save_pm` / `PmData::save_in` (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:310-325) rewrites the **whole document** on every mutation, and each MCP write takes the process-wide `STORE_LOCK` (ops.rs:21) and does its own load-mutate-save cycle (ops.rs:59). So 30 edits means 30 full file rewrites.\n\nMeanwhile the pm GUI, if open, is watching that file — `Sentinel` with a debounce (watch.rs:69) — and reloads on each change. A bulk agent pass currently produces a stream of ~30 reload events in a live GUI. There's a third writer too (the user's editor), and reconciliation is only the 5×12ms retry loop in `load_in` (pm.rs:270).\n\nA batch call collapses that to one load, one save, one watch event.\n\n## Shape\nOne tool taking a list of operations — status changes, field edits, comments, creations — applied under a single lock acquisition and a single save.\n\nTwo things to decide:\n- **Atomicity.** All-or-nothing, or best-effort with a per-operation result? All-or-nothing is easier to reason about and matches the single-save implementation naturally. Best-effort needs a result array and partial-failure semantics. Recommend atomic, since the save is atomic anyway (temp file + rename, pm.rs:314-317).\n- **Creations inside a batch.** `create_ticket` returns a new id, and `next_id` is assigned at creation (pm.rs:377). If a batch creates a ticket *and* references it (e.g. filing PM-85 and immediately linking it from PM-4), the caller can't know the id in advance. Either forbid creations in batches, or support local placeholder ids resolved at apply time. Worth noting this gets sharper once PM-50's ticket links exist.\n\n## Related\n- **PM-50** — filing a set of linked tickets is exactly the case that wants creations-plus-references in one batch.\n- **PM-86** — every save rewrites the whole document from the in-memory struct, so fewer saves is also fewer chances to drop unknown fields.\n\n## Verify\nApply a batch of mixed edits and confirm: one `pm.json5` write (not N), correct history entries for every operation with the right author, and — with the GUI open — a single reload rather than a stream of them.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "mcp"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "claude",
      "created": 1788624618,
      "updated": 1788624618,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
      "history": [
