    },
    {
      "id": 95,
      "title": "Provenance: distinguish agent-written tickets from human-written ones (author is a naming convention today)",
      "body": "## The gap\n`Ticket.author` and `Comment.author` are free-form unverified strings (crates/pm-core/src/pm.rs:182, :123 — deliberately, per PM-15). Resolution is `explicit → ~/.pm/config.json author → git user.name → \"unknown\"` (identity.rs:16-28).\n\nSo when an agent passes `author: \"claude\"`, that works entirely by convention. Nothing marks the ticket as machine-written; the string could equally be a person named Claude, and an agent that simply *omits* `author` is attributed to the resolved fallback — which is the repo's git `user.name`, i.e. **it looks like the human wrote it**. That's the default path, and it's the wrong one.\n\nThis backlog demonstrates both halves: it currently holds tickets authored `\"\"`, `\"unknown\"`, `\"reaganiwadha\"` and `\"claude\"`, and the only thing separating the last from the others is that someone chose to type it.\n\n## Why it matters\nNot attribution for its own sake — **trust calibration**. A ticket body asserting `pm.rs:398` might be verified against the code or might be a confident guess. Knowing which actor produced it changes how much you check before acting on it. Same for a status change: an agent closing a ticket because it believes the work shipped is a weaker signal than you closing it.\n\nIt also affects filtering. \"Show me what the agent filed this week\" isn't expressible today.\n\n## Shape\nA structured field alongside the free-form name — something like `via: Human | Agent` (or a client/tool identifier), set by the MCP server rather than by the caller, so it can't be spoofed by simply picking a different `author` string.\n\nKey point: the MCP server **knows** it is the MCP server. It can stamp that on every write it performs without asking the caller to be honest. That's a stronger guarantee than `author` will ever have, and it doesn't require changing PM-15's deliberate decision that names are unverified.\n\nApplies to tickets, comments, and `HistoryEntry` (pm.rs:154-161) — the history is arguably where it matters most, since that's the audit trail.\n\n## Open questions\n- One bit (human/agent), or a client identifier (\"claude-code\", \"cursor\")? The identifier is more useful and more likely to rot.\n- Should the GUI display it — a small marker on agent-written tickets and comments — or is it filter-only? A visible marker is the thing that actually changes how you read a body.\n- Does it belong on `HistoryEntry` for *every* event, or only at creation? Per-event is more honest: a ticket a human filed and an agent then rewrote is a real and common case (most of this backlog, now).\n\n## Related\n- **PM-15** (done) — established that `author` is free and unverified. This doesn't reverse that; it adds a channel that doesn't rely on the caller's honesty.\n- **PM-65** — profile pictures per author; agents would want a distinct visual identity there too.\n- **PM-86** — a new field on `Ticket`/`Comment`/`HistoryEntry`, so it's droppable by an older binary.\n\n## Verify\nFile a ticket through MCP and one through the GUI; confirm the stored provenance differs, that it can't be overridden by passing a misleading `author`, and that tickets written before the field exists render sensibly rather than claiming to be human-authored.",
      "status": "open",
      "priority": "normal",
      "labels": [
        "mcp",
        "tickets"
      ],
      "assignee": null,
      "author": "claude",
      "created": 1788624636,
      "updated": 1788624636,
      "anchors": [],
      "comments": [],
